---
/*
 * DynamicsEngine — the /explore/dynamics explorable.
 *
 * Structure follows CensusEngine: a panel grid, a live `<dl>` summary, and every
 * `<input>` wrapped INSIDE its `<label>`. That last one is not cosmetic — it is
 * what gives the controls accessible names. The dynamics LAB put the value in a
 * `<legend>` and left the sliders unnamed, so a screen reader announces
 * "slider, 1". Mimicking census fixes that by construction.
 *
 * It does NOT copy census's lens chips. Census has three real control groups to
 * switch between; this page has one, so the chips were choosing between
 * "controls" and "a paragraph", and the `data-lit` panel highlighting they drove
 * had nothing to distinguish.
 *
 * HEARTBEAT TOGGLE: gravity off <-> N-body, and "off" means FREE STREAMING, not
 * a frozen frame. An earlier version held the cluster static to avoid "inventing
 * a zero-force model" — but zero force is not invented, it is `x += v dt`, and
 * the stars already carry velocities. Static made the toggle indistinguishable
 * from paused, which is why the page had two controls doing one thing and why
 * `run` had to override the toggle to do anything. Free streaming is both the
 * honest limit and the page's own title: remove the coupling and the cluster
 * comes apart.
 *
 * RUNG: the backend knows ZAMS values and a main-sequence lifetime. Stars here
 * MOVE; they do not evolve. The page states that. Nothing in this component may
 * imply otherwise.
 *
 * NAMING: this is `dynamics`, never "gravax". The browser does not run gravax;
 * it runs a symplectic integrator of the same family. See explore-authoring.
 */
import Math from "../../components/Math.astro";
const m = (tex: string) => tex;
---

<div class="xpl-dyn" data-dyn>
  {/*
    TYPESET ONCE AT BUILD, CLONED BY THE SCRIPT.

    Several readouts are assembled in JS — the banner, the binary legend, the override badge —
    so they cannot use `<Math>` inline the way static copy does. The alternatives were both
    bad: Unicode (M☉, ε, N^(−1/3)) is not the same typography as the rest of the page and
    breaks the moment an exponent is not in the superscript block, and importing KaTeX into the
    client bundle would ship ~270 kB to typeset eight symbols on a page already carrying WebGPU.

    So the symbols are rendered here, at build time, hidden, and the script copies their HTML.
    Zero bundle cost, and the maths is the same KaTeX as everywhere else by construction.
  */}
  <div class="xpl-dyn__texlib" hidden aria-hidden="true">
    <span data-tex="msun"><Math tex={m("M_\\odot")} /></span>
    <span data-tex="eps"><Math tex={m("\\varepsilon")} /></span>
    <span data-tex="epslaw"><Math tex={m("r_h N^{-1/3}")} /></span>
    <span data-tex="dt"><Math tex={m("\\Delta t")} /></span>
    <span data-tex="tcr"><Math tex={m("t_\\mathrm{cr}")} /></span>
    <span data-tex="a"><Math tex={m("a")} /></span>
    <span data-tex="ecc"><Math tex={m("e")} /></span>
    <span data-tex="pc"><Math tex={m("\\mathrm{pc}")} /></span>
  </div>
  <div class="xpl-dyn__grid">
    <figure class="xpl-dyn__panel xpl-dyn__panel--field">
      <figcaption>
        The cluster
        <span class="xpl-dyn__legend">
          {/* A PROPERTY OF THE DRAW, on the frame that counts in it. It sat inside the clock's
              own note behind a "?", which put the unit the whole page keeps time in somewhere
              nobody looks — and made that note a thousand characters long. Here it costs no
              height, because the caption row already exists. */}
          <span class="xpl-dyn__key"
            ><Math tex={m("t_\\mathrm{cr}")} /> = <b data-v="tc0">—</b> Myr</span
          >
          <button type="button" class="xpl-dyn__info" data-info="tcr" aria-expanded="false"
            aria-label="About the crossing time">?</button>
          <span class="xpl-dyn__popover" data-popover="tcr" role="tooltip" hidden>
            <b>The crossing time</b> — how long a typical star takes to cross the cluster, and the
            unit a cluster actually keeps time in: relaxation and segregation happen on multiples
            of it rather than on Myr.
            <br /><br />
            It is not a control. It follows from the cluster you built, roughly
            <Math tex={m("t_\\mathrm{cr} \\sim r_h / v")} /> with
            <Math tex={m("v \\sim \\sqrt{GM/r_h}")} />, so a bigger <Math tex={m("r_h")} /> or a
            lighter cluster gives a slower clock — and changing <Math tex={m("N")} />,
            <Math tex={m("r_h")} /> or <Math tex={m("\\gamma")} /> redraws it.
            <br /><br />
            Fixed at <Math tex={m("t = 0")} /> and never recomputed, so the crossing-time clock
            stays a ruler rather than one that stretches while you read it.
          </span>
          {/* "scroll to zoom" dropped: the HUD carries a zoom slider and a scale bar now, so the
              caption was spending a third of its width on the one affordance that is already
              visible — and with t_cr added it wrapped to three lines. Dragging has no such
              control, so that half stays. */}
          <span>drag to orbit</span>
          <button type="button" class="xpl-dyn__info" data-info="clock" aria-expanded="false"
            aria-label="About the clock">?</button>
          <span class="xpl-dyn__popover" data-popover="clock" role="tooltip" hidden>
            The clock on the frame reads model time twice: in <b>millions of years</b>, and in
            <b>crossing times</b> — how long a typical star takes to cross the cluster. The
            second is the one a cluster actually keeps, because relaxation and segregation
            happen on multiples of it rather than on Myr. It is blank without gravity, where it
            is undefined.
            <br /><br />
            The value of <Math tex={m("t_\\mathrm{cr}")} /> for this draw is in the caption
            above, with its own note — it is a property of the cluster you built, not of the
            clock.
            <br /><br />
            Model time is not wall-clock time. It advances by a fixed number of integrator steps
            per frame, so how fast it runs depends on how quickly this browser can take them —
            and the clock slows on its own once a hard binary forms, so the pair can be watched
            rather than raced past.
            <br /><br />
            <b>Keys.</b> <kbd>space</kbd> runs and pauses, <kbd>R</kbd> restarts the same draw,
            <kbd>N</kbd> draws a new one. They stand down while a slider or a button has focus,
            so the controls keep their own keys.
          </span>
        </span>
      </figcaption>
      {/*
        EVERY CONTROL RIDES ON THE FRAME, in the two corners the cluster does not
        use: transport and clock along the top, the physics toggle bottom-left.
        The cluster is centred and roughly circular, so the corners are the free
        space — and putting the toggle on the canvas rather than in a row beneath
        it is what lets the metrics strip sit directly under the image.

        "gravity" / "no gravity", not the spec's "isolated". `isolated` is the
        right physics term — each star isolated from the others — but it reads as
        a property of the CLUSTER (isolated from a tide? a galaxy?) rather than as
        the force being switched off. Gravity leads, because it is the term being
        added and the one the page is about; "no gravity" is the control.
      */}
      <div class="xpl-dyn__stage">
        <canvas data-field aria-label="Star cluster in projection; brighter, larger points are more luminous stars"></canvas>
        <div class="xpl-dyn__hud xpl-dyn__hud--top">
          <span class="xpl-dyn__modes" role="group" aria-label="Clock and cluster">
            <button type="button" data-play class="is-active">▶ run</button>
            <button type="button" data-reset>↻ reset</button>
            {/* "new draw" belongs beside reset because the two are the same kind of
                action — start over — differing only in whether the seed changes.
                It was across the panel in a toolbar of its own. */}
            <button type="button" data-reseed>↻ new draw</button>
            {/* Full-screen the CLUSTER, not the whole engine. The panel carries its own
                transport, gravity toggle, scale bar and zoom in the HUD, so it stays usable
                alone — and expanding the engine instead would scale the plots up with it,
                which is the opposite of what a bigger cluster is for. */}
            <button type="button" data-expand aria-pressed="false">⛶ expand</button>
          {/* Beside the thing it exports, rather than in a toolbar of its own — this saves the
              cluster, the one on the plot panel saves that panel's numbers. */}
          <button type="button" data-save-png title="Save the cluster as a PNG">⤓ png</button>
          </span>
          {/* TWO LINES, NOT ONE. Both clocks matter — Myr is what a reader has intuition for,
              crossing times is what the cluster actually keeps — but side by side they made a
              pill wide enough to crowd the frame. Stacked, the chip is as wide as its longer
              line instead of their sum. The second line is empty without gravity, where
              t/t_cr is undefined. */}
          <span class="xpl-dyn__clock" data-clock aria-live="off">
            <span data-clock-t>t = 0.0 Myr</span>
            {/* KaTeX for the SYMBOL, rendered at build time, with JS filling only the number —
                census's pattern. It read literally "0.0 t_cr" on the one readout the page asks a
                reader to think in. */}
            <span class="xpl-dyn__clock-sub" data-clock-tcr hidden
              ><output data-v="tcr-now">—</output> <Math tex={m("t/t_\\mathrm{cr}")} /></span
            >
          </span>
        </div>
        <div class="xpl-dyn__hud xpl-dyn__hud--bottom">
          <span class="xpl-dyn__modes" role="group" aria-label="Gravity">
            <button type="button" data-grav="on" aria-pressed="false">gravity</button>
            <button type="button" data-grav="off" class="is-active" aria-pressed="true">no gravity</button>
          </span>
          {/* The scale bar is what makes a frame that moves honest: it reports how
              far the picture now spans, so an expanding cluster reads as expansion
              rather than as a camera trick. Census puts one in the same corner. */}
          {/*
            THE SCALE BAR AND THE ZOOM THAT MOVES IT, together in one corner.
            Scroll-to-zoom stays, but it is a poor primary control: it fights the page scroll,
            has no visible range, and gives no way back to 1x except guessing. The slider shows
            where you are and where the ends are; the bar underneath keeps it honest by
            reporting what the frame now spans.
          */}
          <span class="xpl-dyn__viewctl">
            <span class="xpl-dyn__scale" aria-hidden="true">
              <span class="xpl-dyn__scale-bar" data-scalebar></span>
              <span class="xpl-dyn__scale-label" data-scalelabel>1 pc</span>
            </span>
            {/* LOGARITHMIC. The renderer clamps zoom to [0.15, 40], nearly two and a half
                decades — linear travel would spend most of the slider above 20x. The value IS
                log10(zoom), so the midpoint is 1x. */}
            <input
              class="xpl-dyn__zoom"
              type="range"
              data-zoom
              min="-0.82"
              max="1.6"
              step="0.01"
              value="0"
              aria-label="Zoom the cluster view"
            />
          </span>
        </div>
        {/*
          THE BINARY BANNER — the page's own limit, announced rather than discovered.

          This run's energy error does not come from everywhere: it comes from one hard
          binary in the core that the fixed step can no longer follow. Measured on seed
          2028, the tightest pair goes from 20551 steps per orbit to 76, and |dE/E| steps
          up with it. That is a real result about star clusters AND the point where this
          model runs out, so the page says which is happening while it happens.

          It rides ON the canvas because vertical space is the binding constraint of this
          layout — a banner in the flow would cost a row on every run, including the ones
          that never form a binary.
        */}
        <div class="xpl-dyn__alert" data-alert role="status" hidden>
          <strong data-alert-title></strong>
          <span data-alert-body></span>
          <button type="button" class="xpl-dyn__alert-btn" data-zoom-pair>show me</button>
        </div>
      </div>
      <small class="xpl-dyn__hint" data-hint>paused — press run</small>
      {/*
        SHOWN ONLY WHEN THE SOFTENING IS NOT THE PAGE'S OWN.

        `?eps=` is a diagnostic knob, and a diagnostic knob that changes the physics silently
        would leave the paragraph below the figure — which states ε = 0.1 r_h N^(−1/3), about
        0.009 pc at N = 400 — asserting a softening the run is not using. That is exactly the
        class of quiet untruth the rest of this component is built against, so the override
        announces itself and states the value actually in force.
      */}
      <small class="xpl-dyn__hint xpl-dyn__hint--eps" data-epsnote hidden></small>
    </figure>

    {/*
      THE LEGEND IS THE KEY, AND IT CARRIES THE EXPLANATION BEHIND A "?".

      What the two curves ARE was a three-line paragraph under the plot, which is
      the one place a reader is not looking while watching them move — and it cost
      more vertical space than the metrics strip. A legend puts the key on the
      figure, and census's `data-info` / `data-popover` pair (its spatial-profile
      note) is the pattern for the prose behind it: it pops OVER the panel rather
      than pushing it taller, so the plot keeps its height.

      The swatches take their colours from the same `--spec-*` tokens the canvas
      reads through `css()`, so a legend cannot end up a different colour from the
      line it labels.
    */}
    <figure class="xpl-dyn__panel">
      <figcaption>
        {/* TABS, not a third panel. A separate panel per curve is what pushed the
            energy strip off the same screen as the cluster; tabs keep the
            footprint fixed and let each plot be full size. The energy strip stays
            OUTSIDE them deliberately — it is the receipt that vouches for whichever
            tab you are on, and behind a tab it could not do that job. */}
        <span class="xpl-dyn__modes" role="group" aria-label="Plot">
          <button type="button" data-plot-tab="radii" class="is-active" aria-pressed="true">
            half-mass radius
          </button>
          <button type="button" data-plot-tab="bound" aria-pressed="false">bound fraction</button>
          <button type="button" data-plot-tab="mf" aria-pressed="false">mass function</button>
          <button type="button" data-plot-tab="lagr" aria-pressed="false">Lagrangian radii</button>
          {/*
            CONTEXTUAL — it appears when a binary is confirmed and leaves when the binary does.
            The pair does not form until well into the run (measured across four draws: t/t_cr
            7.9, 8.3, 9.1, 14.1), so a permanent tab would sit blank for the best part of a
            minute, and a tab that is empty when you press it teaches you not to press it.
          */}
          <button type="button" data-plot-tab="binary" aria-pressed="false" data-pair-tab hidden>
            the binary
          </button>
        </span>
        <span class="xpl-dyn__legend">
          <span class="xpl-dyn__keys" data-legend="radii">
            <span class="xpl-dyn__key"
              ><i class="xpl-dyn__swatch xpl-dyn__swatch--hi"></i>heaviest 10%
              <b data-v="rhi">—</b></span
            >
            <span class="xpl-dyn__key"
              ><i class="xpl-dyn__swatch xpl-dyn__swatch--lo"></i>lightest half
              <b data-v="rlo">—</b></span
            >
          </span>
          <span class="xpl-dyn__keys" data-legend="bound" hidden>
            <span class="xpl-dyn__key"
              ><i class="xpl-dyn__swatch xpl-dyn__swatch--hi"></i>by mass
              <b data-v="legend-bm">—</b></span
            >
            <span class="xpl-dyn__key"
              ><i class="xpl-dyn__swatch xpl-dyn__swatch--lo"></i>by number
              <b data-v="legend-bn">—</b></span
            >
          </span>
          <span class="xpl-dyn__keys" data-legend="lagr" hidden>
            <span class="xpl-dyn__key"><i class="xpl-dyn__swatch xpl-dyn__swatch--hi"></i>20%</span>
            <span class="xpl-dyn__key"><i class="xpl-dyn__swatch xpl-dyn__swatch--lo"></i>50%</span>
            <span class="xpl-dyn__key"><i class="xpl-dyn__swatch xpl-dyn__swatch--e"></i>90%</span>
          </span>
          <span class="xpl-dyn__keys" data-legend="binary" hidden>
            <span class="xpl-dyn__key"
              ><i class="xpl-dyn__swatch xpl-dyn__swatch--hi"></i>this pair
              <b data-v="legend-pair">—</b></span
            >
            <span class="xpl-dyn__key"
              ><i class="xpl-dyn__swatch xpl-dyn__swatch--e"></i>after an exchange</span
            >
            <span class="xpl-dyn__key">now <b data-v="legend-pairnow">—</b></span>
          </span>
          <span class="xpl-dyn__keys" data-legend="mf" hidden>
            <span class="xpl-dyn__key"
              ><i class="xpl-dyn__swatch xpl-dyn__swatch--born"></i>born
              <b data-v="legend-born">—</b></span
            >
            <span class="xpl-dyn__key"
              ><i class="xpl-dyn__swatch xpl-dyn__swatch--kept"></i>still bound
              <b data-v="legend-kept">—</b></span
            >
            <span class="xpl-dyn__key"><i class="xpl-dyn__swatch xpl-dyn__swatch--hi"></i>the law</span>
          </span>
          <button type="button" class="xpl-dyn__info" data-save-csv
            title="Save this plot's numbers as CSV">⤓</button>
          <button
            type="button"
            class="xpl-dyn__info"
            data-info="plot"
            aria-expanded="false"
            aria-label="About this plot"
          >?</button>
          <span class="xpl-dyn__popover" data-popover="plot" role="tooltip" hidden>
            <span data-note="radii">
              Each curve is the half-mass radius of its own group, divided by that group's value at
              <Math tex={m("t = 0")} />, so both start at 1. The split is fixed at the first frame,
              so the same stars are followed all run and the curves cannot separate by relabelling
              who counts as heavy. With gravity on, both are measured about the bound cluster's own
              centre and over its bound stars — a dissolving cluster recoils, and its escapers would
              otherwise dominate the radius.
              <br /><br />
              <b>A gap on its own is not the evidence.</b> With gravity off the curves separate
              anyway, because the two groups begin at different radii and with different speeds —
              and by how much is an accident of the draw. Measured across eight consecutive draws,
              the gap after eight crossing times ran from <Math tex={m("-22\\%")} /> to
              <Math tex={m("+40\\%")} />, with the heavy group spreading faster in three of them. What gravity does is
              different in kind: the heavy curve drops <em>below</em> 1 while the light one climbs.
              Left running, the heavy curve settles well below half its starting radius while the
              light one keeps climbing. How far, and how fast, is not a fixed number: the heaviest
              10% is a few dozen stars, so the curve is noisy from one sample to the next.
            </span>
            <span data-note="bound" hidden>
              The fraction of the cluster still gravitationally bound, by <b>mass</b> and by
              <b>number</b>. They disagree, and the gap is the point: the heaviest stars carry most
              of the mass and are the last to leave, so the mass curve stays high while the star
              count falls away beneath it. Run it long enough and the two separate by tens of
              percent — most of the mass still bound while a third or more of the stars have
              gone.
              <br /><br />
              It does not start at 100%, and that is the initial conditions rather than a bug: the
              velocities are an isotropic Maxwellian scaled to a virial ratio of
              <Math tex={m("Q = 1/2")} />, which is not the equilibrium distribution function of
              the density profile they are paired with. A Maxwellian's tail is unbounded, so a few
              stars begin above the local escape speed. Measured across eight consecutive draws,
              92% to 98% of the mass and 89% to 97% of the stars are bound at
              <Math tex={m("t = 0")} />.
              <br /><br />
              Both are blank with gravity off. Boundness is measured against a potential that is not
              being integrated then, so a number there would be a counterfactual rather than a
              property of what you are watching.
            </span>
            <span data-note="lagr" hidden>
              The radii enclosing <b>20%</b>, <b>50%</b> and <b>90%</b> of the bound cluster's
              mass — the standard way a cluster's evolution is read, because the inner and outer
              parts do opposite things and a single half-mass radius shows neither.
              <br /><br />
              <b>A cluster comes apart from the outside in.</b> Encounters move energy outward:
              the inner region gives it up and stays compact, while the stars that receive it
              move onto wider and wider orbits. Measured here over twenty crossing times, the
              90% radius grew about three and a half times while the half-mass radius grew by
              roughly half and the 20% radius hardly moved. The <em>spread</em> between the
              curves is the result, not any one of them.
              <br /><br />
              The inner curve is the noisy one — it is a few dozen stars, and it wanders. Read
              the gap between top and bottom rather than any single wiggle. With gravity off all
              three simply expand together, which is the honest picture of a cloud with nothing
              holding it: no redistribution, so no differentiation.
            </span>
            <span data-note="binary" hidden>
              The core binary's own orbit: <b>semi-major axis</b> across, <b>eccentricity</b> up,
              one point per sample since it formed. Hardening moves it <em>left</em>. The colour
              changes at an <b>exchange</b> — where a passing star swapped itself in for one of
              the pair — so a jump in the track is a change of binary rather than a change of
              orbit.
              <br /><br />
              <b>The scatter is real, and it is not noise in the measurement.</b> Both numbers are
              INSTANTANEOUS, computed from the pair's relative energy at that moment, and that
              energy is perturbed by the rest of the cluster and softened at small separations. So
              a single unchanging orbit still draws a cloud rather than a point: measured over four
              draws, <Math tex={m("a")} /> ranged over a factor of 1.7 to 2.9 within one pair's
              own track. The cloud is smoothed nowhere — a tidier line would be a claim about a
              Keplerian orbit that this pair is not on.
              <br /><br />
              Eccentricity is high but not uniformly so: median <Math tex={m("e")} /> across those
              four draws ran from 0.50 to 0.94. A binary like this forms by capture rather than in
              place, so it arrives on a long thin orbit — and the damage to the energy strip is
              done in the small part of that orbit spent near periapsis.
              <br /><br />
              The tab is here only while there is a binary. It appears once a pair has held
              together for half a crossing time and leaves if that pair is broken up or replaced;
              across four draws the pair first formed between 8 and 14 crossing times in.
            </span>
            <span data-note="mf" hidden>
              The mass function <b>as drawn</b> (grey) against the stars <b>still bound</b>
              (teal), in counts per dex, with the law they were sampled from over both.
              <br /><br />
              <b>The gap is dynamical, and it is one-sided.</b> A cluster evaporates from the
              bottom up: energy is shared out by encounters, the lightest stars end up moving
              fastest, and they are the ones that reach escape speed. So the low-mass bins
              hollow out while the heavy end sits there. Measured on this cluster at twenty
              crossing times, about seven in ten of the lightest stars were still bound against
              nine in ten above a solar mass.
              <br /><br />
              <b>Two honest limits.</b> The heaviest bins hold a handful of stars — often three
              or four — so their bars are noise, not a trend; the low-mass end is where this
              plot has the numbers to say anything. And the stars here do not age, so this is
              only the <em>dynamical</em> half of the story. A real cluster also loses its most
              massive stars to their own deaths, which empties the opposite end of the diagram
              from the one you are watching.
            </span>
          </span>
        </span>
      </figcaption>
      <div class="xpl-dyn__plot">
        <span class="xpl-dyn__ytitle" data-ytitle="radii"
          ><Math tex={m("r_h(t)\\,/\\,r_h(0)")} /></span
        >
        <span class="xpl-dyn__ytitle" data-ytitle="bound" hidden>bound fraction</span>
        <span class="xpl-dyn__ytitle" data-ytitle="lagr" hidden
          ><Math tex={m("r\\;[\\mathrm{pc}]")} /></span
        >
        <span class="xpl-dyn__ytitle" data-ytitle="mf" hidden
          ><Math tex={m("\\mathrm{d}N/\\mathrm{d}\\log m")} /></span
        >
        <span class="xpl-dyn__ytitle" data-ytitle="binary" hidden
          ><Math tex={m("e")} /></span
        >
        <canvas data-radii aria-label="Cluster evolution: half-mass radius of the heaviest and lightest stars, the bound fraction, or the mass function as drawn against what is still bound"></canvas>
        {/* The x axis is TIME for two tabs and MASS for the third, so it swaps with them —
            a plot labelled t (Myr) over a mass axis is the same class of quiet untruth the
            scheme label guards against. Space-separated list, matched by inclusion. */}
        <span class="xpl-dyn__xtitle" data-xtitle="radii bound lagr"
          ><Math tex={m("t\\;[\\mathrm{Myr}]")} /></span
        >
        <span class="xpl-dyn__xtitle" data-xtitle="mf" hidden
          ><Math tex={m("m\\;[M_\\odot]")} /></span
        >
        <span class="xpl-dyn__xtitle" data-xtitle="binary" hidden
          ><Math tex={m("a\\;[\\mathrm{pc}]")} /></span
        >
      </div>
    </figure>

    {/*
      ONE CONSOLE, TWO ROWS: the knobs on top, the numbers they move underneath.

      Both used to be squeezed into the 496 px cluster column — the sliders wrapped
      to two rows with ~90 px of travel each, and the readout was clipping five of
      its six values. Full width gives four sliders 256 px apiece and the metrics a
      single line, and it puts every control and every number in one place instead
      of two.

      It also fixes the popover clipping at the source: the cluster panel carries
      `overflow: hidden` to clip its canvas, and anything opening from a control
      inside it was cut off. Nothing here needs to be clipped.
    */}
    <div class="xpl-dyn__panel xpl-dyn__panel--strip xpl-dyn__console">
      <div class="xpl-dyn__controls" aria-label="Cluster controls" role="group">
        <label class="xpl-dyn__ctrl">
          <span class="xpl-dyn__ctrl-val"><Math tex={m("N")} /> = <output data-n-val>400</output></span>
          <input type="range" data-n min="100" max="800" step="10" value="400" />
        </label>
        <label class="xpl-dyn__ctrl xpl-dyn__ctrl--noted">
          <span class="xpl-dyn__ctrl-val"
            ><Math tex={m("r_h")} /> = <output data-rh-val>0.65</output> pc</span
          >
          <input type="range" data-rh min="0.3" max="5" step="0.05" value="0.65" />
          <button type="button" class="xpl-dyn__info" data-info="rh" aria-expanded="false"
            aria-label="About the half-mass radius">?</button>
          <span class="xpl-dyn__popover" data-popover="rh" role="tooltip" hidden>
            <b>Half-mass radius</b> — the radius containing half the cluster's mass. It sets the
            profile's scale, and the scale radius <Math tex={m("a")} /> is derived from it, so
            changing <Math tex={m("\\gamma")} /> reshapes the cluster without resizing it.
            <br /><br />
            <b>You get approximately what you ask for, and the difference is the point.</b> This
            slider sets the scale the stars are DRAWN from; the cluster that comes out is one
            finite sample of it. Measured across eight consecutive draws at
            <Math tex={m("N = 400")} />, a requested 0.65 pc produced an actual half-mass radius
            between 0.52 and 0.86 pc.
            <br /><br />
            The reason is the mass function, not the profile. Sorted by <em>number</em> the
            half-radius barely moves — 0.61 to 0.70 pc across the same draws — so the stars are
            being placed correctly. It is the <em>mass</em> weighting that swings: a single
            40 <Math tex={m("M_\\odot")} /> star is about a seventh of a
            270 <Math tex={m("M_\\odot")} /> cluster, so whether it happens to land at 0.04 pc or
            at 1.17 pc moves the half-mass radius on its own.
            <br /><br />
            That propagates: <Math tex={m("t_\\mathrm{cr} \\propto r_h^{3/2}")} />, so the
            crossing time varies by a factor of two between draws that asked for the same
            cluster. It is the same sampling problem the
            <a href="/explore/census">census</a> is about, arriving through a control rather than
            through a histogram.
          </span>
        </label>
        <label class="xpl-dyn__ctrl xpl-dyn__ctrl--noted">
          <span class="xpl-dyn__ctrl-val"
            ><Math tex={m("\\gamma")} /> = <output data-gamma-val>5.0</output></span
          >
          <input type="range" data-gamma min="2.5" max="6" step="0.1" value="5" />
          <button type="button" class="xpl-dyn__info" data-info="gamma" aria-expanded="false"
            aria-label="About the concentration parameter">?</button>
          <span class="xpl-dyn__popover" data-popover="gamma" role="tooltip" hidden>
            <b>Concentration.</b> Stars are placed from an <b>EFF</b> profile (Elson, Fall &amp;
            Freeman 1987), whose three-dimensional density falls as
            <Math tex={m("\\rho(r) \\propto (1 + r^2/a^2)^{-\\gamma/2}")} />.
            <br /><br />
            At <Math tex={m("\\gamma = 5")} /> that expression <em>is</em> the Plummer law.
            Lower <Math tex={m("\\gamma")} /> is a shallower fall-off and a more extended halo
            (<Math tex={m("\\gamma \\approx 3")} /> is typical of a young cluster); higher is
            more concentrated. It reshapes the cluster at fixed
            <Math tex={m("r_h")} />, so the two sliders are independent.
          </span>
        </label>
        <label class="xpl-dyn__ctrl xpl-dyn__ctrl--noted">
          <span class="xpl-dyn__ctrl-val"
            ><Math tex={m("\\alpha")} /> = <output data-alpha-val>2.3</output></span
          >
          <input type="range" data-alpha min="1.5" max="3" step="0.1" value="2.3" />
          <button type="button" class="xpl-dyn__info" data-info="alpha" aria-expanded="false"
            aria-label="About the high-mass slope">?</button>
          <span class="xpl-dyn__popover" data-popover="alpha" role="tooltip" hidden>
            <b>High-mass slope.</b> The power law the mass function follows above about a solar
            mass, <Math tex={m("\\xi(m) \\propto m^{-\\alpha}")} />. Salpeter is 2.35; the
            default here is 2.3.
            <br /><br />
            It is a <em>dynamics</em> knob, not just a population one. Alpha sets how much of the
            cluster's mass sits in its few heaviest stars, and segregation is driven by exactly
            that contrast — flatten it and the heavy tail that does the sinking gets heavier.
            Steepen it and the cluster becomes a crowd of low-mass stars with little to segregate.
          </span>
        </label>
        <label class="xpl-dyn__ctrl xpl-dyn__ctrl--noted">
          <span class="xpl-dyn__ctrl-val">time <output data-scrub-val>live</output></span>
          <input type="range" data-scrub min="0" max="0" step="1" value="0" disabled
            aria-label="Scrub back through the run" />
          <button type="button" class="xpl-dyn__info" data-info="scrub" aria-expanded="false"
            aria-label="About the timeline">?</button>
          <span class="xpl-dyn__popover" data-popover="scrub" role="tooltip" hidden>
            <b>Drag back through the run.</b> The cluster, the curves and the readouts all move to
            the sample you land on, so the moment the binary formed can be looked at rather than
            remembered.
            <br /><br />
            <b>It reads the record; it does not re-run the model.</b> Nothing is integrated
            backwards — these are stored snapshots of states the run actually passed through, and
            the live run is untouched underneath. Let go at the far right and you are back on it.
            <br /><br />
            <b>The record gets coarser as the run gets longer, and that is visible here.</b> The
            history keeps a fixed number of samples: once it is full, pairs of samples are merged,
            so the spacing between what you can reach doubles. Early in a run you can land on
            almost any moment; a long way in, the nearest stored sample may be some way from the
            one you wanted. The gap between ticks is the resolution you actually have.
          </span>
        </label>
        <div class="xpl-dyn__ctrl xpl-dyn__ctrl--noted">
          <span class="xpl-dyn__ctrl-val">integrator</span>
          <span class="xpl-dyn__modes" role="group" aria-label="Integrator">
            <button type="button" data-scheme="fsi4" class="is-active" aria-pressed="true">FSI4</button>
            <button type="button" data-scheme="logh" aria-pressed="false">LogH</button>
            <button type="button" data-scheme="hermite" aria-pressed="false">Hermite</button>
          </span>
          <button type="button" class="xpl-dyn__info" data-info="scheme" aria-expanded="false"
            aria-label="About the integrator choice">?</button>
          <span class="xpl-dyn__popover" data-popover="scheme" role="tooltip" hidden>
            <b>FSI4</b> is a fourth-order <b>symplectic</b> scheme (Forest-Ruth family). Its energy
            error is <em>bounded</em> — it wanders and comes back — which is what lets the strip
            below certify a run at all. Its one weakness is the one this cluster produces: a fixed
            step cannot shorten itself for a hardening binary.
            <br /><br />
            <b>LogH</b> fixes exactly that, and it is the interesting one. A symplectic scheme
            cannot simply be given a step controller — its bounded error comes <em>from</em> the
            fixed step, which is what makes it conserve one nearby Hamiltonian instead of a
            different one each step. So LogH changes the problem instead: it integrates in a
            <em>fictitious</em> time <Math tex={m("s")} /> with
            <Math tex={m("\\mathrm{d}t/\\mathrm{d}s = 1/(-U)")} />, so a fixed step in
            <Math tex={m("s")} /> gives a step in <Math tex={m("t")} /> that shortens itself
            wherever the potential deepens — symplectic <em>and</em> adaptive, with no criterion
            anywhere in it (Mikkola &amp; Tanikawa 1999; Preto &amp; Tremaine 1999).
            <br /><br />
            On a two-body orbit it is spectacular: measured over twenty orbits at eccentricity
            0.99, FSI4's peak error is <Math tex={m("1.7\\times10^{2}")} /> — the orbit is
            destroyed — against <Math tex={m("4.2\\times10^{-11}")} /> for LogH at the same step
            count. On <em>this</em> cluster it is merely better,
            <Math tex={m("4\\times")} /> to <Math tex={m("24\\times")} /> depending on the
            draw, because the transformation is global:
            one binary is diluted among four hundred stars. Resolving that properly needs per-pair
            regularisation, which the research codes do and a browser does not.
            <br /><br />
            <b>Hermite</b> is also fourth order and also adapts, but by a different route: it
            <em>measures</em> a step from each star's acceleration and jerk (Aarseth's criterion)
            and takes the smallest. That is a controller, and it costs it the symplectic
            property — its energy error is <b>secular</b>, growing steadily rather than
            returning.
            <br /><br />
            <b>So the three differ in what adapts, and in what the error does.</b> Worst
            <Math tex={m("|\\Delta E/E|")} /> so far, measured on this cluster at this page's own
            settings:
            <table class="xpl-dyn__cmp">
              <thead>
                <tr><th></th><th>adapts?</th><th>2</th><th>10</th><th>15</th><th>20</th></tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td>FSI4</td><td>no</td><td><Math tex={m("1.3\\times10^{-6}")} /></td><td><Math tex={m("1.1\\times10^{-5}")} /></td><td><Math tex={m("3.0\\times10^{-4}")} /></td><td><Math tex={m("3.0\\times10^{-4}")} /></td></tr>
                <tr><td>LogH</td><td>by transformation</td><td><Math tex={m("2.6\\times10^{-6}")} /></td><td><Math tex={m("3.5\\times10^{-5}")} /></td><td><Math tex={m("6.1\\times10^{-5}")} /></td><td><Math tex={m("6.1\\times10^{-5}")} /></td></tr>
                <tr><td>Hermite</td><td>by criterion</td><td><Math tex={m("5.7\\times10^{-8}")} /></td><td><Math tex={m("2.8\\times10^{-6}")} /></td><td><Math tex={m("1.6\\times10^{-5}")} /></td><td><Math tex={m("3.5\\times10^{-5}")} /></td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
            Read the last two columns rather than the first: <b>the two symplectic schemes stop
            getting worse</b> — <Math tex={m("3.0\\times10^{-4}")} /> and
            <Math tex={m("6.1\\times10^{-5}")} />, unchanged from fifteen crossing times to
            twenty — while Hermite is still climbing, <Math tex={m("1.6\\times10^{-5}")} /> to
            <Math tex={m("3.5\\times10^{-5}")} />. That is "bounded" and
            "secular" showing up as numbers rather than as adjectives.
            <br /><br />
            Which means <em>Hermite is the most accurate here and the least trustworthy to
            extrapolate</em>. Its small number does not promise to stay small; theirs do. FSI4
            is the default despite being the least accurate of the three on this configuration,
            because it is the one ported directly from my <code>gravax</code> code — so what you
            are watching is the scheme the research code runs, not the best result a browser
            could produce.
          </span>
        </div>
      </div>

      {/* Each label is its own note trigger — a "?" per metric would be seven icons
          in a row, so the label carries the affordance instead. */}
      {/* aria-live="off", like the clock above and for the same reason: every one of these
        seven values is rewritten each frame, so "polite" queues an announcement per frame
        and a screen reader never finishes the first. The figures are readable on demand;
        a live region is for something that changes occasionally. */}
    {/*
      THE METRICS BAR IS GONE, and every number in it is still on the page — beside the curve
      it describes rather than in a row of eight cells that belonged to nothing in particular.

        r_h hi, r_h lo   -> the half-mass tab's legend
        |dE/E|           -> the energy strip's legend
        bound M, bound N -> the bound-fraction tab's legend (moved earlier)
        t, t/t_cr        -> the clock on the cluster, which is where time was already shown
        t_cr             -> the clock's own note, since it is a property of the draw

      Its popovers moved with them. This is also what makes the four plot tabs fit on one row:
      the grid's height runs about C + 0.75 x W2, and dropping this band takes C down far
      enough that the column can be widened to the 660 px the tabs need while the whole engine
      still fits an 857 px screen. The bar was costing the page twice.
    */}

      {/*
        THE BINARY'S OWN NUMBERS, and this row EXISTS ONLY WHILE THERE IS A BINARY.

        A contextual row rather than six more cells in the strip above: most of a run has no
        hard pair, and six permanently-empty readouts would cost a line of the scarcest thing
        in this layout to say "nothing here". It appears with the banner and leaves with it.

        One shared note rather than a "?" per metric, unlike the strip above — these six all
        describe the SAME object, so six separate explanations would repeat each other five
        times.
      */}
      <dl class="xpl-dyn__stats xpl-dyn__stats--pair" data-pair-stats aria-live="off" hidden>
        <div class="xpl-dyn__pairlabel">
          <dt><button type="button" data-info="m-pair" aria-expanded="false">hard binary</button></dt>
          <dd>
          <span class="xpl-dyn__popover" data-popover="m-pair" role="tooltip" hidden>
            The most bound pair in the cluster, once it has held together for half a crossing
            time — long enough to be a binary rather than two stars passing.
            <br /><br />
            <b>a</b> is the semi-major axis and <b>e</b> the eccentricity of their orbit about
            each other; <b>P</b> is its period. <b>hard</b> is the binding energy in units of a
            typical star's kinetic energy — above 1 the pair is "hard", meaning encounters
            tighten it further instead of breaking it up, which is why the number climbs.
            <br /><br />
            <b>steps/orbit</b> is the one that explains the energy strip. It is how many
            integrator steps fall in one orbit, and a fixed step needs of order a hundred to
            follow periapsis. Watch it fall as <b>a</b> shrinks: that is the model running out,
            and the error below rising is the consequence.
            <br /><br />
            It reads <b>—</b> under <b>Hermite</b>, and that is the honest answer rather than a
            missing feature: Hermite sizes every sub-step from the local acceleration and jerk,
            so there is no one step to count an orbit in. <b>LogH</b> does report one — its
            physical step floats with the depth of the potential — so the figure there is the
            step it actually took, and you should expect it to hold up far better than FSI4's
            as the pair tightens. That is the adaptivity, visible as a number.
            <br /><br />
            Expect <b>e</b> to be large. A binary like this forms by capture rather than in
            place, so it arrives on a long thin orbit and hardens by repeated close passages —
            which is also why the damage is concentrated in the small part of the orbit spent
            near periapsis.
            <br /><br />
            <b>"Show me" draws the orbit as well as framing it.</b> The curve is the lighter
            star's path around the pair's <em>centre of mass</em> — the two stars both orbit that
            point, and it is itself moving through the cluster, so the trail is drawn in its frame
            or the ellipse would be smeared along the binary's own path. Every point on it is a
            state the integrator produced: it is sampled every step rather than every eighth
            frame, because by the time this matters one orbit takes tens of steps, and nothing
            between the samples is filled in.
            <br /><br />
            {/*
              HEGGIE'S LAW, in the note the `hard` figure already lives in rather than as a
              seventh "?" — these six readouts describe one object, which is why they share one
              note (see the header above). Author-year only, matching the citation style of the
              concentration and integrator notes; Heggie D. C., 1975, MNRAS, 173, 729 and Hills
              J. G., 1975, AJ, 80, 809, verified against their records rather than recalled.
            */}
            <b>Why it tightens instead of breaking up — Heggie's law.</b> The rule for what
            encounters do to a binary is one of the oldest results in stellar dynamics:
            <b>hard binaries get harder, soft binaries get softer</b> (Heggie 1975; Hills 1975).
            A soft pair — more loosely bound than a passing star's kinetic energy — is pulled
            apart by the next encounter. A hard one is not: on average a passing star leaves it
            <em>more</em> tightly bound than it found it, and carries the difference away as
            kinetic energy.
            <br /><br />
            So a hard binary never settles. It keeps giving energy to the cluster around it, which
            is why the <b>hard</b> figure above only ever climbs, and why a single binary can heat
            a whole cluster. It is also why this one eventually outruns the timestep: nothing in
            the physics stops it tightening, so the only question is whether the arithmetic can
            keep up.
          </span></dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt><Math tex={m("m_1{+}m_2")} /></dt>
          <dd><span data-v="pair-m">—</span> <Math tex={m("M_\\odot")} /></dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt><Math tex={m("a")} /></dt>
          <dd><span data-v="pair-a">—</span> pc</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt><Math tex={m("e")} /></dt>
          <dd data-v="pair-e">—</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt><Math tex={m("P")} /></dt>
          <dd><span data-v="pair-p">—</span> <Math tex={m("\\mathrm{Myr}")} /></dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>hard</dt>
          <dd><span data-v="pair-hard">—</span><Math tex={m("\\times")} /></dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>steps/orbit</dt>
          <dd data-v="pair-steps">—</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>

    {/* The energy strip is the RECEIPT, not a co-star. On the lab, drift is the
        subject; here it is the evidence that the run above can be trusted, so it
        is persistent and small rather than a fourth equal panel. */}
    <figure class="xpl-dyn__panel xpl-dyn__panel--energy">
      <figcaption>
        Energy drift
        <span class="xpl-dyn__legend">
          <span class="xpl-dyn__key" data-schemename>FSI4 · bounded</span>
          <span class="xpl-dyn__key"
            ><i class="xpl-dyn__swatch xpl-dyn__swatch--e"></i><Math tex={m("|\\Delta E/E|")} />
            <b data-v="drift">—</b></span
          >
          <button
            type="button"
            class="xpl-dyn__info"
            data-info="energy"
            aria-expanded="false"
            aria-label="About the energy drift trace"
          >?</button>
          <span class="xpl-dyn__popover" data-popover="energy" role="tooltip" hidden>
            Fractional energy error against the tolerance this run is checked at (the dashed line).
            For the first several crossing times it sits near <Math tex={m("10^{-9}")} />, then
            climbs in <b>steps</b> — and each step has a cause the panel above names: a
            <b>hard binary</b> in the core. Measured here, the tightest pair went from about
            20,000 integrator steps per orbit to under 100 as it hardened, and the error jumped
            each time it tightened, sitting flat in between. Discrete jumps with no creep between
            them is what a symplectic scheme should do. <b>If the trace crosses the dashed line
            the run ends</b> — past it the arithmetic is no longer solving these equations well
            enough for anything above to mean anything, and continuing would just be a
            convincing-looking picture of nothing.
            <br /><br />
            The reverse does not follow. A conserved energy says the integrator solved the equations
            it was given; it says nothing about whether those equations still describe a star
            cluster. See the note under the figure for where the model runs out.
            <br /><br />
            With gravity off the strip stays empty: there is no integrator running to make a claim
            about.
          </span>
        </span>
      </figcaption>
      {/* LABELLED, like the plot above it. The strip shipped with a bare canvas:
          its y axis is decades of a dimensionless ratio and its x axis is Myr,
          and neither was stated anywhere on the figure — a reader had to infer
          both from the caption. Same KaTeX titles as the r_h plot, so the two
          figures name their axes the same way. */}
      <div class="xpl-dyn__plot xpl-dyn__plot--strip">
        <span class="xpl-dyn__ytitle"><Math tex={m("|\\Delta E/E|")} /></span>
        <canvas data-energy aria-label="Fractional energy error against time"></canvas>
        <span class="xpl-dyn__xtitle"><Math tex={m("t\\;[\\mathrm{Myr}]")} /></span>
      </div>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <noscript>
    <p class="xpl-dyn__noscript">
      This explorable integrates a star cluster in your browser, so it needs JavaScript. The
      physics it demonstrates is described in the text below.
    </p>
  </noscript>
</div>

<script>
  import {
    scenario,
    chooseIntegrator,
    createConservationMonitor,
    measure,
    lagrangianRadii,
    centreOfMass,
    pairResolution,
    hardestBoundPair,
    createPairWatch,
    createStepGuard,
    type StepGuard,
  } from "@novascope/core/dynamics";
  /*
   * The renderer is named directly, never through the `viz` barrel — the barrel
   * is three-free so the node gates can import the pure modules, exactly as
   * viz/starfield/index.ts documents for its own.
   *
   * `toRenderModel` is CENSUS'S selector, not a copy of it. Both pages get their
   * cluster's size, colour and alpha from that one function, which is what
   * "the ONE physics→pixel mapping" in state/render.ts means and what stops the
   * two clusters drifting into two different-looking objects.
   */
  /*
   * THE STATE IS THE ADDRESS. ADR 0012 committed to "(seed, params, t) — reproducible and
   * URL-shareable" and this page had none of it: every cluster on it was unreachable except by
   * setting five sliders in the right order. The codec is `core/params/urlState`, already built
   * and build-gated for the star lab; this is the second consumer, not a second copy.
   *
   * It matters most where the page is going: a lecture wants one link per concept so the demo
   * cannot be fumbled live, a talk becomes a set of bookmarks, and a bug report from a stranger
   * arrives as a URL that reproduces the state exactly.
   */
  import {
    decode,
    encode,
    enumField,
    numberField,
    boolField,
  } from "@novascope/core/params/urlState";
  import { createClusterPoints, type ClusterPoints } from "@novascope/viz/clusterPoints";
  import { toRenderModel, toIMFHistogram, type IMFModel } from "@novascope/state";
  /* The SAME histogram census draws, given a second bar series — see viz/histogram.ts.
     A private copy here would drift from it in ticks, margins and log handling. */
  import { renderHistogram } from "@novascope/viz/histogram";
  /* The LARGE pair, not the one census's H–R diagram and IMF histogram use: these
     two plots are full-width, where 14px reads small. See the note in axis.ts.
     Both are LITERAL font strings on purpose — also documented there, and this
     page is the reason that note exists. */
  import { AXIS_FONT_LG, SUB_FONT_LG, niceTicks, drawPower } from "@novascope/viz/axis";

  const root = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-dyn]");
  if (root) {
    const $ = <T extends HTMLElement>(sel: string): T => root.querySelector<T>(sel)!;

    const field = $<HTMLCanvasElement>("[data-field]");
    const radiiCv = $<HTMLCanvasElement>("[data-radii]");
    const energyCv = $<HTMLCanvasElement>("[data-energy]");
    const nInput = $<HTMLInputElement>("[data-n]");
    const nVal = $<HTMLOutputElement>("[data-n-val]");
    const rhInput = $<HTMLInputElement>("[data-rh]");
    const rhVal = $<HTMLOutputElement>("[data-rh-val]");
    const alphaInput = $<HTMLInputElement>("[data-alpha]");
    const alphaVal = $<HTMLOutputElement>("[data-alpha-val]");
    const gammaInput = $<HTMLInputElement>("[data-gamma]");
    const gammaVal = $<HTMLOutputElement>("[data-gamma-val]");
    const playBtn = $<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-play]");
    const clockT = $<HTMLElement>("[data-clock-t]");
    const clockTcr = $<HTMLElement>("[data-clock-tcr]");
    const hint = $<HTMLElement>("[data-hint]");
    const driftEl = $<HTMLElement>("[data-v=\"drift\"]");
    const scaleBar = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-scalebar]");
    const zoomInput = root.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>("[data-zoom]");
    /** Which evolution curve the shared plot canvas is showing. */
    let plotTab: "radii" | "bound" | "mf" | "lagr" | "binary" = "radii";
    const scaleLabel = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-scalelabel]");
    const summary = $<HTMLElement>("[data-summary]");

    const gravBtns = Array.from(root.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-grav]"));

    /*
     * FULL-SCREEN THE CLUSTER PANEL.
     *
     * `requestFullscreen` returns a promise that REJECTS when the document is not allowed to
     * enter it — an iframe without `allowfullscreen`, or a call not driven by a gesture. An
     * unhandled rejection there would surface as a console error and nothing else, so the
     * failure is caught and the button simply stays as it was.
     *
     * Nothing here resizes the renderer: `clusterPoints` already listens for `resize`, and
     * entering or leaving full screen fires one.
     */
    const fieldPanel = $<HTMLElement>(".xpl-dyn__panel--field");
    const expandBtn = $<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-expand]");
    const isExpanded = () =>
      document.fullscreenElement === fieldPanel || fieldPanel.classList.contains("is-expanded");
    const syncExpand = () => {
      const on = isExpanded();
      expandBtn.textContent = on ? "⛶ close" : "⛶ expand";
      expandBtn.setAttribute("aria-pressed", String(on));
    };
    /*
     * NATIVE FULL SCREEN IF ALLOWED, A FIXED OVERLAY IF NOT.
     *
     * `requestFullscreen` is refused in more places than it is granted: an embedded frame
     * without the permission, an iframe missing `allowfullscreen`, a browser preview pane. And
     * it refuses in TWO different shapes — some engines reject the promise, others THROW
     * SYNCHRONOUSLY, which is what this one does ("TypeError: Permissions check failed"). A
     * lone `.catch()` handles the first and misses the second, which is exactly how the first
     * version of this button did nothing at all: the throw escaped before the catch attached.
     *
     * So the call is wrapped, and the fallback is not an error message but the feature: a
     * fixed, inset-0 overlay that no permission can refuse. The reader gets a full-window
     * cluster either way, and only the browser chrome differs.
     */
    const expandFallback = (on: boolean) => {
      fieldPanel.classList.toggle("is-expanded", on);
      /* The renderer sizes itself from `resize`, which a class change does not fire. */
      window.dispatchEvent(new Event("resize"));
      syncExpand();
    };
    expandBtn.addEventListener("click", () => {
      if (isExpanded()) {
        if (document.fullscreenElement === fieldPanel) {
          try {
            void document.exitFullscreen()?.catch(() => {});
          } catch {
            /* already gone */
          }
        }
        expandFallback(false);
        return;
      }
      try {
        const p = fieldPanel.requestFullscreen?.();
        if (p) void p.catch(() => expandFallback(true));
        else expandFallback(true);
      } catch {
        expandFallback(true);
      }
    });
    /* Fires for Escape and for the browser's own controls too, so the label cannot get stuck
       reading "close" on a panel that has already left full screen. */
    document.addEventListener("fullscreenchange", syncExpand);
    /* Escape leaves the fallback as well, since nothing else would. */
    document.addEventListener("keydown", (e) => {
      if (e.key === "Escape" && fieldPanel.classList.contains("is-expanded")) expandFallback(false);
    });

    /*
     * Scoped to ROOT, not to `summary`.
     *
     * It queried inside the main `<dl data-summary>`, which silently did nothing for the
     * binary row — that is a SIBLING `<dl>`, so `summary.querySelector` found none of its
     * cells and every value stayed at the placeholder "—" while the row itself dutifully
     * appeared. `if (el)` made the failure silent, which is right for a readout that may not
     * exist and is exactly why this was invisible until the numbers were read back.
     */
    const setStat = (key: string, v: string) => {
      const el = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>(`[data-v="${key}"]`);
      if (el) el.textContent = v;
    };
    /*
     * A typeset symbol's HTML, from the build-time library above.
     *
     * Cached because these are read on the frame path — the binary legend rewrites every eighth
     * frame — and a querySelector per symbol per write is work for nothing.
     */
    const texCache = new Map<string, string>();
    const tex = (name: string): string => {
      const hit = texCache.get(name);
      if (hit !== undefined) return hit;
      const el = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>(`[data-tex="${name}"]`);
      const html = el ? el.innerHTML : "";
      texCache.set(name, html);
      return html;
    };
    /* Same as setStat, for a readout that carries typeset symbols. The interpolated values are
       this file's own formatted numbers, never anything a reader supplies. */
    const setStatHTML = (key: string, html: string) => {
      const el = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>(`[data-v="${key}"]`);
      if (el) el.innerHTML = html;
    };

    /* ── state ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
    let gravity = false;
    /*
     * WHICH INTEGRATOR. FSI4 is the default and the honest one for this page;
     * Hermite is offered as a comparison instrument. See the note by the control:
     * Hermite is fourth order but NOT symplectic, so its energy error is secular
     * rather than bounded — which is the property the strip's argument rests on.
     */
    let scheme: "fsi4" | "logh" | "hermite" = "fsi4";
    let playing = false;
    let run: ReturnType<typeof build> | null = null;
    /* History of (t, r_h heavy, r_h light) and of fractional energy error. */
    let hist: {
      t: number; hi: number; lo: number; e: number; bm: number; bn: number;
      /* Lagrangian radii of the BOUND cluster [pc] — 20/50/90% of its mass. */
      l20: number; l50: number; l90: number;
    }[] = [];
    /* Halving threshold, not a cap on the run: see the decimation in sample().
       600 samples is already ~4x the horizontal pixels of the plot at 1440px. */
    const MAX_SAMPLES = 600;
    /*
     * A POSITION SNAPSHOT PER STORED SAMPLE, so the timeline moves the CLUSTER and not just the
     * curves. Without it a "timeline" would scrub two plots while the picture above them stayed
     * on the present — which reads as the run rewinding when it has not.
     *
     * Cost is bounded by the same MAX_SAMPLES the history is: 600 x N x 3 floats, which is
     * 2.9 MB at N = 400 and 5.8 MB at the slider's maximum of 800. Float32 rather than Float64
     * on purpose — this is display state, and the integrator's own Float64 positions are never
     * touched by it.
     */
    let histPos: Float32Array[] = [];
    /** Which stored sample the reader is looking at, or null when live. */
    let scrubIndex: number | null = null;
    /* A cluster property, not a per-star one — a coeval cluster is chemically
       uniform (explore-authoring rule 2). Solar; the dynamics scenario carries no
       composition of its own, so stating it here is stating an assumption rather
       than reading one. */
    const CLUSTER_Z = 0.02;
    /*
     * STEP SIZE, AND WHY IT IS t_cross/128 RATHER THAN /64.
     *
     * The scheme was never the problem — this page runs FSI4, fourth order and
     * symplectic, confirmed from the scenario's own `defaultScheme`. The step was.
     * Measured on this cluster, N = 400, worst |dE/E| over ten crossing times:
     *
     *   dt = t_cross/64    3.95e-4      <- what shipped
     *   dt = t_cross/128   7.46e-6      53x better
     *   dt = t_cross/256   2.91e-7
     *
     * A 53x gain for one halving is far steeper than the 16x a fourth-order scheme
     * gives in its asymptotic regime, which is the tell: /64 was not a slightly
     * coarse step, it was outside the range where the scheme's order applies at
     * all. /128 puts it back inside, and the error drops two orders below the
     * monitor's 1e-3 trust limit instead of sitting a factor of two under it.
     *
     * SUBSTEPS keeps the clock rate unchanged: two half-steps per frame advance
     * the same model time per frame as one whole one did, so the cluster evolves
     * at the pace it always did and only the cost goes up (2x the force
     * evaluations — measured in the browser before relying on it).
     */
    /*
     * MEASURED HERE, not taken from `stepsForSoftening`. That helper derives the
     * step from the softening and is right down to fraction 0.25, but it
     * under-predicts below: at 0.1 it returns 405, where the measured drift is
     * 2.96e-1 — six orders past this page's tolerance. 2048 is what fraction 0.1
     * actually needs (1024 drifts 1.24e-3; 2048 holds at 4.38e-5). See the
     * validated-range note on `stepsForSoftening`.
     */
    const STEP_DIVISOR = 2048;
    /* The divisor actually in force. Set from the URL on load; never from a control. */
    let stepDivisor = STEP_DIVISOR;
    /* Opt-in: shorten the step as the drift climbs instead of halting. Set from the URL. */
    let autostep = false;
    /*
     * Rebuilt with every run, because its whole state is a statement about THIS trajectory —
     * carrying a scale over from the last cluster would start a fresh run at a step it never
     * earned, and the strip would be certifying a run at a resolution nothing had justified.
     */
    let stepGuard: StepGuard | null = null;
    const SUBSTEPS = 8;
    /*
     * SOFTENING, and why it is a tenth of the mean interparticle spacing.
     *
     * eps = 0.5 r_h N^(-1/3) is half the mean spacing — a sensible COLLISIONLESS
     * choice, and the wrong one for a page about two-body relaxation, because it
     * smooths away the close encounters that drive the segregation. Measured over
     * forty crossing times, the light/heavy radius ratio it produced was 2.33x
     * against 7.87x at a fifth of the softening. The knob was suppressing the
     * effect the page exists to show.
     *
     * eps and dt are coupled: a smaller softening means faster encounters and
     * needs a finer step. At 0.1 the step has to reach t_cross/2048 to stay
     * honest — /1024 drifts 1.24e-3 and would breach the limit below.
     */
    /*
     * THE PAGE'S OWN VALUE, and the default of the `?eps=` knob below.
     *
     * That knob exists because this number is exactly the kind of claim that should be
     * checkable by running it rather than by believing a note. eps and dt are coupled, so
     * lowering it does not simply "resolve encounters better" — the run needs a finer step to
     * stay honest, and the page's own trust limit is what says whether it got one. Turning the
     * knob down and watching what the limit does is a measurement; a sentence predicting it is
     * not.
     *
     * It is URL-only rather than a slider on purpose: it changes what the page's prose says
     * about itself, so it announces the override (see `data-epsnote`) instead of quietly
     * contradicting the paragraph under the figure.
     */
    const SOFTENING_FRACTION = 0.1;
    /*
     * TIGHTER THAN THE MONITOR'S 1e-3 DEFAULT, which was calibrated for
     * t_cross/128 where the honest drift is 5e-6..1.4e-5. At /2048 with this
     * softening the worst measured over forty crossing times is 4.38e-5, so 1e-4
     * is roughly twice the worst honest value rather than seventy times it — a
     * line that would actually catch something.
     */
    const ENERGY_TOLERANCE = 1e-4;
    let hi0: number | null = null;
    let lo0: number | null = null;
    /*
     * THE MASS FUNCTION AS DRAWN, frozen at t = 0, and which stars are still bound.
     *
     * Binned by `toIMFHistogram` against the scenario's OWN identity — the same law the
     * sampler drew from — so the bars and the analytic line over them cannot come from
     * different distributions. `state/render.ts` documents why that would be an artefact
     * rather than sampling noise, and `scenarios.ts` exposes `identity` for exactly this.
     */
    let bornIMF: IMFModel | null = null;
    /*
     * THE OVERLAY IS NOT REBUILT EVERY FRAME.
     *
     * Binning the bound stars means allocating a LatentStar per star, filtering, and running
     * `toIMFHistogram` — measured, doing that per frame roughly halved the frame rate on this
     * tab against the others. It is also pointless at that rate: escape is a relaxation-time
     * process, so the histogram barely moves between one frame and the next.
     *
     * Recomputed every MF_EVERY frames, and immediately whenever something has actually
     * changed underneath it — a rebuild, or arriving on the tab.
     */
    const MF_EVERY = 8;
    let mfCounts: number[] | null = null;
    let mfStale = true;
    let mfFrame = 0;
    /* Reused from the diagnostics pass `sample()` already runs, rather than a second O(N^2)
       `measure()` for the plot: boundness is expensive and is already known each frame. */
    let boundFlags: Uint8Array | null = null;
    /** Elapsed model time [Myr]. Advanced by BOTH integration paths — see sample(). */
    let tSim = 0;
    /** Model time between STORED history samples; grows as the run outlives the buffer. */
    let storeEvery = 0;
    /** Peak |dE/E| since the last stored sample — see the push in sample(). */
    let worstSinceStore = 0;
    let lastStored = -Infinity;

    /* Bumped by "new draw" only. Reset keeps the seed, so "reset" means "same
       cluster from t=0" and "new draw" means "a different cluster" — two
       distinct things that were previously the same button twice. */
    let seed = 2026;
    /* The softening actually in force. Set from the URL on load; never from a control. */
    let softeningFraction = SOFTENING_FRACTION;

    /*
     * THE URL SCHEMA. Ranges mirror the controls' own `min`/`max` so a hand-edited or stale
     * link is CLAMPED into something runnable rather than rejected — the codec's own rule, and
     * the reason a bookmark survives a range being narrowed later.
     *
     * `t` is deliberately absent. The run is a trajectory, not a coordinate: restoring it would
     * mean integrating to that time on load, which is seconds of work and — since the page's own
     * measurements show late-time states are not reproducible across engines — would not even
     * land on the same cluster. A link carries the DRAW and the settings; the reader presses run.
     */
    const URL_SCHEMA = {
      seed: numberField(0, 999999, 2026, 0),
      n: numberField(100, 800, 400, 0),
      rh: numberField(0.3, 5, 0.65, 2),
      gamma: numberField(2.5, 6, 5, 1),
      alpha: numberField(1.5, 3, 2.3, 1),
      scheme: enumField(["fsi4", "logh", "hermite"] as const, "fsi4"),
      tab: enumField(["radii", "bound", "mf", "lagr", "binary"] as const, "radii"),
      gravity: boolField(false),
      /*
       * SOFTENING FRACTION — a diagnostic, deliberately reachable only by URL.
       *
       * Down to 0.001, a hundredth of the page's own value, because the interesting question is
       * what happens BELOW the shipped setting and a range that stopped at it could not ask.
       * Clamped like every other field, so a hand-typed 0 opens at 0.001 rather than dividing
       * by zero in the force model.
       */
      eps: numberField(0.001, 1, 0.1, 4),
      /*
       * STEPS PER CROSSING TIME — the control for the `eps` experiment above.
       *
       * eps and dt are COUPLED: lowering the softening makes encounters faster and needs a
       * finer step. With only `eps` reachable, dropping it halts the run and there is no way to
       * tell "this softening is unusable" from "this softening needs a finer step". This is the
       * knob that separates them.
       *
       * Powers of two from 256 to 32768; 2048 is what fraction 0.1 was measured to need
       * (1024 drifts 1.24e-3, 2048 holds at 4.38e-5). Values are clamped, not rejected.
       */
      steps: numberField(256, 32768, 2048, 0),
      /*
       * SHORTEN THE STEP RATHER THAN STOP — off by default, and that is a pedagogical choice
       * rather than a technical one.
       *
       * The page's best moment is that the cluster builds a binary and the binary breaks the
       * arithmetic. A run that quietly survived it would delete the lesson, so the default keeps
       * the fixed step and the honest halt. This mode is the answer to the question the halt
       * raises — what does a real code DO about it — and it is opt-in.
       */
      autostep: boolField(false),
    };

    /* `replaceState`, never `pushState`: dragging a slider must not fill the back button with
       a hundred history entries. The address bar is the shareable artefact, not the history. */
    const syncUrl = () => {
      const query = encode(URL_SCHEMA, {
        seed,
        n: Number(nInput.value),
        rh: Number(rhInput.value),
        gamma: Number(gammaInput.value),
        alpha: Number(alphaInput.value),
        scheme,
        tab: plotTab,
        gravity,
        eps: softeningFraction,
        steps: stepDivisor,
        autostep,
      });
      history.replaceState(null, "", query ? `?${query}` : location.pathname);
    };

    function build() {
      const s = scenario("cluster");
      const built = s.build({
        n: Number(nInput.value),
        seed,
        gamma: Number(gammaInput.value),
        rHalfPc: Number(rhInput.value),
        alphaHigh: Number(alphaInput.value),
        softeningFraction,
      });
      /*
       * The scenario's OWN default scheme, not a faster one chosen here.
       * Forcing leapfrog measured 1.3e-2 energy drift by ten crossing times, so
       * `healthy` latched false and the page's own "stop believing this"
       * threshold had been crossed while the page went on presenting the result.
       * The scenario knows which schemes are honest for its configuration;
       * overriding that was the bug.
       *
       * Those figures were taken against the THEN tolerance of 1e-3 and the then
       * softening; both have since moved (ENERGY_TOLERANCE is 1e-4, the softening
       * fraction 0.1), so they are kept as the record of why this line exists
       * rather than as current numbers. What FSI4 does now is measured below on
       * SOFTENING_FRACTION.
       */
      const picked = chooseIntegrator(built.state, built.force, {
        prefer: scheme,
        /* Hermite is the only one of the two that can size its own sub-steps; the
           flag is ignored by FSI4, which has no adaptive mode by construction. */
        adaptive: scheme === "hermite",
        maxStep: built.timeUnit / stepDivisor,
      });
      /* Mass split is fixed once per draw, so the two curves follow the SAME
         stars all run — a split recomputed each frame would track whichever
         stars happen to be heavy now and could not show sinking. */
      const idx = Array.from({ length: built.state.n }, (_, i) => i).sort(
        (a, b) => built.state.mass[b] - built.state.mass[a],
      );
      const nHeavy = Math.max(1, Math.round(idx.length * 0.1));
      const heavy = new Set(idx.slice(0, nHeavy));
      const light = new Set(idx.slice(Math.floor(idx.length * 0.5)));
      return {
        built,
        integrator: picked.integrator,
        monitor: createConservationMonitor(picked.integrator, {
          energyTolerance: ENERGY_TOLERANCE,
        }),
        heavy,
        light,
      };
    }

    /*
     * TIME IS THE PAGE'S OWN ACCUMULATOR; the monitor supplies the crossing-time
     * clock and the drift.
     *
     * It used to be the monitor's `s.t` for all three, on the reasoning that a
     * separate accumulator would be a second clock that could disagree. That was
     * right while the integrator was the only thing advancing the state — and it
     * broke the moment "no gravity" became free streaming, because that path
     * never calls `integrator.step()` and so never advances the monitor's `t`.
     * The symptom was the clock reading 0.0 Myr while the cluster visibly
     * dispersed, and the radius plot collapsing every sample onto t = 0.
     *
     * There is exactly one accumulator and both integration paths advance it by
     * the same dt, so the two cannot disagree. tCross and energyDrift still come
     * off the monitor, and both are only meaningful with gravity on.
     */
    function sample() {
      if (!run) return;
      const st = run.built.state;
      /*
       * THE MONITOR IS ONLY POLLED WHEN THE INTEGRATOR IS RUNNING.
       *
       * `sample()` latches `worst` and `breached` from an energy it recomputes
       * each call. During free streaming that energy changes for a reason that is
       * not integrator error — no force is being integrated — so polling it armed
       * the health latch against a run it was not watching: measured, `healthy`
       * went false by 2 crossing times of free streaming. Invisible today only
       * because every route back to gravity rebuilds the monitor, which is a
       * coincidence of control flow rather than a guarantee. It also cost an
       * O(N^2) energy evaluation per frame in the one mode that needs none.
       *
       * IT IS NO LONGER POLLED **HERE**, THOUGH — `frame()` polls it once per
       * INTEGRATOR STEP and this reads the latest of those. See the note there.
       * `latest` is null only before anything has stepped (`rebase` clears it), so
       * the fallback covers the reading `rebuild()` takes at t = 0.
       */
      const s = gravity ? (run.monitor.latest ?? run.monitor.sample()) : null;
      /*
       * BOUNDNESS IS A GRAVITY QUANTITY, so it is only measured when gravity is
       * acting. `measure` computes each star's energy against the force model's
       * potential; during free streaming that potential is not being integrated,
       * so a "bound fraction" there is a counterfactual — it answers "what would
       * be bound IF gravity were on", presented as a live property. (It also
       * costs two O(N^2) passes per frame in the one mode that needs none.)
       */
      const d = gravity ? measure(st, run.built.force, tSim) : null;
      boundFlags = d ? d.bound : null;
      if (++mfFrame % MF_EVERY === 0) mfStale = true;
      /*
       * RADII ABOUT THE BOUND CLUSTER, AND OVER BOUND STARS ONLY.
       *
       * Both halves were wrong and both mattered by ~100 crossing times:
       *
       *  - About the ORIGIN, `lagrangianRadii` measured displacement as much as
       *    size. Momentum is conserved so the whole system's centre stays put, but
       *    the bound remnant recoils against its escapers: measured, |COM_bound|
       *    reaches 12.3 pc by 800 t_cross while r_h(heavy) read 11.6 pc.
       *
       *  - Over ALL members of a subset, the light curve became an escaper
       *    diagnostic. Measured at t/t_cr = 1064: r_h(light) = 92.7 pc unfiltered
       *    against 21.8 pc over bound light stars. Its late rise was half the
       *    light subset's mass sitting beyond 90 pc — stars that had left, not a
       *    cluster that had grown. `diagnostics.ts` documents this exact filtered
       *    call as the intended use.
       *
       * With gravity off there is no bound set and no cluster to centre on, so
       * both fall back to every star about the origin — which is the honest
       * description of a cloud that is simply dispersing.
       */
      /* Idempotent now that `frame()` folds every step's drift in as it takes it —
         `s` is the last of those. It is kept for the one reading that does NOT come
         through the step loop: the sample `rebuild()` takes at t = 0. */
      if (s) worstSinceStore = Math.max(worstSinceStore, s.energyDrift);
      const boundOnly = d ? (i: number) => d.bound[i] === 1 : null;
      const centre = d ? centreOfMass(st, boundOnly!).position : undefined;
      const inSet = (set: Set<number>) => (i: number) =>
        set.has(i) && (!boundOnly || boundOnly(i));
      const hi = lagrangianRadii(st, [0.5], inSet(run.heavy), centre)[0];
      const lo = lagrangianRadii(st, [0.5], inSet(run.light), centre)[0];
      /* All three fractions from ONE call: `lagrangianRadii` sorts once and reads the
         cumulative mass off that sort, so asking for three costs no more than asking for one.
         Over the WHOLE cluster, not a mass subset — this tab is about structure, where the
         heavy/light split above is about segregation. */
      const [l20, l50, l90] = lagrangianRadii(st, [0.2, 0.5, 0.9], boundOnly ?? undefined, centre);
      /* Baseline on the FIRST sample so both curves start at exactly 1.
         Absolute r_h of the heaviest 10% is a ~40-star estimate and lands well
         off the light-star value at t=0 by sampling noise alone — measured 0.48
         vs 0.70 pc with no primordial segregation in the ICs at all
         (params.ts defaults segregation to 0). Plotting the raw radii would
         show the curves already apart before gravity had done anything, which
         reads as segregation that was imposed. The claim is about CHANGE, so
         the plot shows change; the absolute radii stay in the readout below. */
      if (hi0 === null) hi0 = hi || 1;
      if (lo0 === null) lo0 = lo || 1;
      /* Energy drift is a claim ABOUT THE INTEGRATOR, so it is only recorded
         while the integrator is running. Free streaming never calls it: the
         monitor would still difference kinetic against a potential nothing is
         solving, report a large and growing "drift", and the strip would tell the
         reader to stop believing a panel that is perfectly fine. NaN breaks the
         trace rather than drawing a false one — drawSeries already skips
         non-finite samples. */
      /*
       * KEEP THE WHOLE RUN AT UNIFORM RESOLUTION IN TIME.
       *
       * Two earlier versions of this were wrong, in opposite ways.
       *
       * `shift()` past 600 samples dropped the OLDEST point while the x-axis still
       * spanned 0..t_now, so the curve detached from the left edge and crawled
       * rightward across an empty plot.
       *
       * Halving the array (`filter(i % 2 === 0)`) was meant to fix that, and the
       * comment here claimed "the record thins, it does not truncate". THAT WAS
       * FALSE, and measurably so. Halving thins everything already stored while
       * fresh samples keep arriving at full rate, so a sample that has survived k
       * halvings sits at spacing 2^k while the newest sit at spacing 1 —
       * exponentially biased against the past. Simulated over the 72,300 frames of
       * an 821 Myr run: 600 samples retained, and the first EIGHTY PERCENT of the
       * timeline held exactly ONE of them, with a 789.7 Myr gap between the first
       * two. The plot drew a point at the origin, a straight line across most of
       * the run, and all of its detail in the last 4% — which is what "the energy
       * plot loses most of the line" was.
       *
       * The fix is to thin the INPUT as well as the record: store a sample only
       * every `storeEvery` of model time, and after each halving reset that
       * interval to the spacing the surviving samples actually have. The record
       * then stays uniform in time at every length, and the readouts below still
       * update every frame — only the history is thinned.
       */
      if (tSim - lastStored >= storeEvery) {
        hist.push({
          t: tSim,
          hi: hi / hi0,
          lo: lo / lo0,
          /*
           * THE WORST SINCE THE LAST STORED SAMPLE, not the instantaneous value.
           *
           * The monitor latches a breach every frame while the history stores one
           * sample every `storeEvery`, so a spike between stores could turn the
           * readout red and appear nowhere in the trace — a plot that disagreed
           * with the verdict beside it. A comment here previously claimed the two
           * "cannot drift apart"; taking the max over the interval is what
           * actually makes that true.
           */
          e: s ? worstSinceStore : NaN,
          /* Same rule as the drift: boundness is a gravity quantity, so with the
             force switched off there is nothing to record rather than a zero. */
          bm: d ? d.boundMassFraction : NaN,
          bn: d ? d.boundFraction : NaN,
          l20: l20!,
          l50: l50!,
          l90: l90!,
        });
        histPos.push(Float32Array.from(st.pos));
        lastStored = tSim;
        worstSinceStore = 0;
      }
      if (hist.length > MAX_SAMPLES) {
        /*
         * MERGED IN PAIRS, not filtered — `e` is a MAX OVER AN INTERVAL, so dropping the odd
         * samples throws their peaks away rather than thinning the record. Over a long run
         * the plotted envelope then loses exactly the spikes the strip exists to show, which
         * is the same aliasing as the once-per-frame sampling this file already fixed, one
         * layer up. Confirmed in the browser: on a long run the trust-limit line was not
         * drawn at all, because the PLOTTED maximum never came within a decade of it while
         * the monitor's own peak was 5.8e-5.
         *
         * The other fields are smooth in time, so the survivor's value is representative;
         * only the error envelope needs the max.
         */
        const mergeE = (x: number, y: number): number =>
          Number.isFinite(x) ? (Number.isFinite(y) ? Math.max(x, y) : x) : y;
        const merged: typeof hist = [];
        for (let i = 0; i < hist.length; i += 2) {
          const a = hist[i]!;
          const b = hist[i + 1];
          merged.push(b ? { ...a, e: mergeE(a.e, b.e) } : a);
        }
        hist = merged;
        /* The SURVIVOR's snapshot, matching the merge above index for index — if these two ever
           fell out of step the timeline would show one moment's cluster against another
           moment's curves, which is the worst possible failure for a control whose whole job is
           to line them up. */
        const mergedPos: Float32Array[] = [];
        for (let i = 0; i < histPos.length; i += 2) mergedPos.push(histPos[i]!);
        histPos = mergedPos;
        const span = hist[hist.length - 1]!.t - hist[0]!.t;
        /* Measured from what survived, not doubled blindly, so the interval can
           never drift out of step with the array it is meant to describe. */
        storeEvery = hist.length > 1 ? span / (hist.length - 1) : storeEvery * 2;
      }

      /* Both clocks in one chip on the frame. t/t_cr is omitted rather than shown as "—"
         when there is no gravity: it is undefined there, and a dash inside a running clock
         reads as a broken readout rather than an absent quantity. */
      clockT.textContent = `t = ${tSim.toFixed(1)} Myr`;
      /* The symbol is typeset in the markup; only the number is written here, so the readout
         cannot lose its units to a string edit. Hidden rather than emptied without gravity —
         t/t_cr is undefined there, and a bare "—" inside a running clock reads as broken. */
      clockTcr.hidden = !s;
      if (s) setStat("tcr-now", s.tCross.toFixed(1));
      setStat("rhi", `${hi.toFixed(2)} pc`);
      setStat("rlo", `${lo.toFixed(2)} pc`);
      /*
       * BOTH FRACTIONS, because they disagree and the difference is the story.
       * `boundMassFraction` is the quantity the literature quotes and it is
       * dominated by a handful of heavy stars that stay put — measured 65% by mass
       * against 52.5% by NUMBER at the same instant, because the heaviest 40 carry
       * 61.7% of the cluster's mass. Labelled bare, the mass figure reads as "most
       * of the cluster is still there" while half the stars have gone.
       */
      /*
       * THE SCALE IS FIXED. The frame is the scenario's `viewPc` and it does not
       * move, ever.
       *
       * An earlier version grew the frame to keep the cluster inside it. That is
       * the wrong trade on this page: a cluster expanding thirtyfold would look
       * motionless with only the scale bar changing, and the expansion is the
       * physics — hiding it inside the camera is precisely the kind of render
       * trick this series is not allowed to use. Stars leaving the frame IS the
       * result.
       *
       * What replaces it is under the reader's control rather than the page's:
       * scroll to zoom out, with the scale bar reporting what the frame now
       * spans. That shows the same thing without the picture quietly redefining
       * its own units while you watch.
       *
       * The frame still CENTRES on the bound remnant, which is not a scale change:
       * without it a recoiling cluster simply exits the fixed frame sideways and
       * the panel goes blank for a reason that has nothing to do with expansion.
       */
      /*
       * FOLLOWING THE PAIR IS A MODE, not a one-shot jump.
       *
       * "show me" used to call setFraming once. This line then overwrote the centre on the
       * VERY NEXT FRAME, so the picture zoomed to the binary's scale and immediately snapped
       * back to the cluster's centre — leaving the reader staring at a tiny empty patch of
       * core. Anna reported exactly that: "i don't see the binary when i clicked show me."
       *
       * A binary also MOVES — it orbits in the cluster potential and recoils — and it
       * HARDENS, so its own scale shrinks while you watch. Both have to be tracked or it
       * drifts out of frame within a crossing time.
       */
      if (followPair && pair) {
        /*
         * CENTRE ONLY — THE SCALE IS SET ONCE, when follow mode is entered.
         *
         * This passed `radiusPc: 3a` every frame, and `a` is computed from the pair's
         * INSTANTANEOUS relative energy, which oscillates around the orbit. So the frame
         * rescaled a few percent every frame and the scale bar visibly jittered. Anna: "don't
         * let it self-zoom after you zoom into the binary, it causes the scale to jitter."
         *
         * It is the same rule as the cluster frame's, and for the same reason: a picture that
         * quietly redefines its own units while you watch is not showing you the physics. The
         * pair does shrink in frame as it hardens — that IS the physics — and the reader can
         * zoom if they want a closer look.
         */
        const st = run.built.state;
        const mi = st.mass[pair.i];
        const mj = st.mass[pair.j];
        const mT = mi + mj;
        points?.setFraming({
          centre: [
            (st.pos[pair.i * 3] * mi + st.pos[pair.j * 3] * mj) / mT,
            (st.pos[pair.i * 3 + 1] * mi + st.pos[pair.j * 3 + 1] * mj) / mT,
            (st.pos[pair.i * 3 + 2] * mi + st.pos[pair.j * 3 + 2] * mj) / mT,
          ],
        });
      } else if (centre) points?.setFraming({ centre: Array.from(centre) });
      updateScaleBar();

      /* Into the plot legends, not the strip. Blank without gravity, for the same reason the
         curves are: boundness measured against a potential nothing is integrating would be a
         counterfactual. */
      setStat("legend-bm", d ? `${(d.boundMassFraction * 100).toFixed(0)}%` : "—");
      setStat("legend-bn", d ? `${(d.boundFraction * 100).toFixed(0)}%` : "—");
      driftEl.textContent = s ? s.energyDrift.toExponential(1) : "—";
      /* `healthy` latches false once drift has ever breached tolerance, so this
         cannot flicker back to reassuring after a bad excursion. */
      driftEl.classList.toggle("is-bad", !!s && !run.monitor.healthy);

      /*
       * CROSSING THE TRUST LIMIT ENDS THE RUN.
       *
       * The page has always claimed that "if it crosses that line the arithmetic has stopped
       * being trustworthy and nothing above it means anything" — and then carried on
       * integrating and drawing, which made the sentence decorative. A limit a page states and
       * then ignores teaches a reader that limits are decorative.
       *
       * `healthy` is LATCHING, so this fires on the first breach and cannot be un-fired by a
       * later good sample; the run stops at the moment the claim stopped being true, not at
       * some point after it recovered.
       *
       * MEASURED 2026-08-08, after the monitor began sampling every STEP (see frame()). Twelve
       * consecutive draws at N = 400, dt = t_cross/2048, run to 25 crossing times in node:
       *
       *   ends on the trust limit         5   at t/t_cr 5.9, 6.7, 7.3, 8.4, 19.2
       *   ends on an unresolvable binary  3   at 13.1, 16.8, 21.9
       *   runs the full 25                4
       *
       * This note previously read "roughly a third of reachable draws ... around fifteen to
       * twenty-five crossing times". Both halves came from the once-per-frame sampling this file
       * has since abandoned, which aliased the peaks away: FOUR OF THE FIVE energy breaches are
       * before ten crossing times, not after fifteen. The old parenthetical measuring the halved
       * step is dropped for the same reason rather than kept as a number nothing now supports.
       *
       * AND THE EXACT TIME DOES NOT TRANSFER TO A READER'S BROWSER. Same seed, initial conditions
       * bit-identical to 21 digits, node vs Chrome: worst |dE/E| agrees to eight significant
       * figures at 8,640 steps and then diverges — node peaks at 1.017e-4 and halts, Chrome never
       * exceeds 5.82e-5. The peak is set by individual close passages, so round-off is amplified
       * chaotically. Hence the page states the MECHANISM and never a rate; keep it that way.
       *
       * Halving the step would cost twice the wall time, which is the trade Anna declined;
       * ending the run is the honest alternative to running past the limit.
       */
      if (s && !run.monitor.healthy && !halted) {
        const why = pairConfirmed && pair
          ? ` The ${pair.masses[0].toFixed(0)}+${pair.masses[1].toFixed(0)} ${tex("msun")} binary ` +
            `below is almost certainly why: at ${pair.stepsPerOrbit.toFixed(0)} steps per orbit ` +
            `its periapsis is no longer resolved.`
          : "";
        /*
         * THE PEAK, NOT THE LATEST SAMPLE — and this only became wrong when the monitor
         * started being polled every step.
         *
         * `breached` latches on the step that crossed the line, but `latest` is the LAST
         * step of the frame, and the error is a narrow spike: by the end of the frame it
         * has already fallen back. Measured on seed 2028, which breaches at a peak of
         * 2.2e-4 — `latest` there is 4.7e-6, so the banner would have read "the energy
         * error reached 4.7e-6, crossing the line", quoting a number forty times UNDER
         * the limit it says was crossed.
         *
         * `worst.energy` is the max across every sample, so it is the value that actually
         * tripped the latch.
         */
        halt(
          "Stopped — past the trust limit",
          `The energy error reached ${run.monitor.worst.energy.toExponential(1)}, crossing the line this ` +
            `run is checked against. Beyond it the integration is no longer solving the ` +
            `equations accurately enough to mean anything, so the run ends rather than showing ` +
            `you a cluster it has stopped computing.${why} Reset or draw again.`,
        );
      }
    }

    /*
     * ADAPTIVE SCALE BAR — the thing that makes a moving frame honest.
     *
     * Same rule census uses in `renderClusterField`: pick a round length that is
     * about 22% of the frame, from a 1/2/5 ladder. Drawn as DOM rather than into
     * the canvas because the canvas is WebGPU and has no text; the HUD is already
     * an overlay, so it costs nothing extra.
     */
    const NICE_PC = [
      0.01, 0.02, 0.05, 0.1, 0.2, 0.5, 1, 2, 5, 10, 20, 50, 100, 200, 500, 1000,
    ];
    function updateScaleBar() {
      if (!points || !scaleBar) return;
      /* The wheel changes the renderer's zoom directly, so the slider is re-read FROM it
         rather than treated as the source of truth — otherwise the two disagree the moment
         anyone scrolls. This runs wherever the framing changed, which is exactly when. */
      if (zoomInput && document.activeElement !== zoomInput) {
        zoomInput.value = String(Math.log10(points.zoom));
      }
      const cssPerPc = points.pxPerPc / Math.min(2, window.devicePixelRatio || 1);
      if (!(cssPerPc > 0)) return;
      const target = field.clientWidth * 0.22;
      let Lpc = NICE_PC[0]!;
      for (const c of NICE_PC) if (c * cssPerPc <= target) Lpc = c;
      scaleBar.style.inlineSize = `${(Lpc * cssPerPc).toFixed(1)}px`;
      /* One branch: `${Lpc} pc` reads correctly either side of a parsec (0.05 pc, 2 pc), and
         the ladder above has no value needing another form. This was a ternary with two
         identical arms. */
      if (scaleLabel) scaleLabel.textContent = `${Lpc} pc`;
    }

    /* ── drawing ───────────────────────────────────────────────── */
    const fit = (cv: HTMLCanvasElement) => {
      const dpr = Math.min(window.devicePixelRatio || 1, 2);
      const r = cv.getBoundingClientRect();
      cv.width = Math.max(1, Math.round(r.width * dpr));
      cv.height = Math.max(1, Math.round(r.height * dpr));
      const ctx = cv.getContext("2d")!;
      ctx.setTransform(dpr, 0, 0, dpr, 0, 0);
      return { ctx, w: r.width, h: r.height };
    };

    const css = (name: string, fb: string) =>
      getComputedStyle(document.documentElement).getPropertyValue(name).trim() || fb;

    /*
     * The cluster is drawn by `viz/clusterPoints` — three.js / WebGPU, the stack
     * ADR 0015 chose — fed by CENSUS'S OWN SELECTOR.
     *
     * Two separate things had to be right here, and conflating them cost a
     * session's worth of knob-turning:
     *
     * 1. THE STACK. The raw-WebGL engine sized its box from the 98th percentile
     *    of this cluster's radii, and the radial distribution has a heavy tail
     *    (p50 0.67 pc, p98 4.69, max 13.47), so the box came out at 15 pc and 400
     *    stars rendered as 87 resolvable blobs around a saturated core. ADR 0015
     *    records the same failure on the same engine family and defers the port
     *    until there is evidence; this was that evidence.
     *
     * 2. THE LOOK, which is NOT a property of the stack. `viz/starfield` draws a
     *    photograph — apparent size is the quad containing a real PSF — and no
     *    exposure setting turns that into census's diagram. Measured on this
     *    cluster, the photographic path drew the heaviest 10% at 19.4x the
     *    typical star (flares over an empty frame) and the unit-luminance path at
     *    1.7x (a flat haze with the segregation invisible).
     *
     * `toRenderModel` is the answer to (2) and it already existed. It is census's
     * selector, called here rather than reimplemented, so both pages' clusters
     * come from one law: sizePx from log-luminosity compressed into 0.6-5 px,
     * alpha floored at 0.55 so the faint majority never disappears, framing on
     * the 90th-percentile radius — which is, independently, the fix for (1).
     *
     * It also satisfies explore-authoring rule 1 by construction: `toRenderModel`
     * derives every star's state through `star()`, so nothing here reaches past
     * the contract.
     */
    let points: ClusterPoints | null = null;

    /**
     * The integrator's state as LatentStar[] — the shape `toRenderModel` takes.
     *
     * Z is the cluster's, not the star's: a coeval cluster is chemically uniform
     * (explore-authoring rule 2), which is also why it is not carried per particle
     * by the dynamics state.
     */
    function latentStars() {
      const st = run!.built.state;
      const out = [];
      for (let i = 0; i < st.n; i++) {
        out.push({
          id: i,
          mass: st.mass[i]!,
          Z: CLUSTER_Z,
          x: st.pos[3 * i]!,
          y: st.pos[3 * i + 1]!,
          z: st.pos[3 * i + 2]!,
          vx: st.vel[3 * i]!,
          vy: st.vel[3 * i + 1]!,
          vz: st.vel[3 * i + 2]!,
        });
      }
      return out;
    }

    /*
     * `toRenderModel` SORTS its output (painter's order, render.ts), so the model's
     * k-th star is NOT the k-th particle of the integrator state. `renderOrder[k]`
     * is the state index the k-th drawn instance belongs to.
     *
     * THIS IS THE MAPPING THAT WAS MISSING, and its absence was the worst bug on
     * the page. `positions()` returned `st.pos` in state order while the renderer
     * had packed sizes and colours in SORTED order, so every frame each instance
     * was moved to a different star's position. Measured: 400 of 400 instances
     * mismatched, mean displacement 1.51 pc on a cluster with r_h = 0.55 pc. The
     * biggest, bluest points were drawn wherever, which means "the heaviest stars
     * sink" — the entire claim of this page — was never visible in the canvas,
     * from the first frame.
     *
     * It was invisible to me because the frame still looked like a plausible
     * cluster: the right stars with the wrong coordinates is a correct-looking
     * picture of nothing.
     */
    let renderOrder: number[] = [];
    /*
     * THE ALPHA EACH STAR WAS GIVEN BY `toRenderModel`, kept so the escaper fade can be a
     * MULTIPLIER on it rather than a replacement. The selector floors alpha at 0.55 precisely
     * so the faint majority never disappears; overwriting it here would undo that and make
     * this page's cluster a different object from census's.
     */
    let baseAlpha = new Float32Array(0);
    /*
     * How far an UNBOUND star is faded. It is not zero, and that is the point: a star that has
     * escaped has not stopped existing, it has stopped belonging — and watching them stream
     * away is half of what the bound-fraction and mass-function tabs are counting.
     */
    const ESCAPER_ALPHA = 0.25;
    /** Half-width of the view [pc] — the scenario's own `viewPc`, FIXED. See sample(). */
    let frameR = 0;

    /* t = 0 always: at this rung a star MOVES but does not evolve, so asking
       toRenderModel for an age would claim evolution the backend cannot compute.
       The page says so in as many words. */
    function renderModel() {
      const model = toRenderModel(latentStars(), { t: 0, selectedId: null, toggles: {} });
      renderOrder = model.stars.map((st) => st.id); // latentStars() sets id = state index
      baseAlpha = Float32Array.from(model.stars, (st) => st.alpha);
      return model;
    }

    /**
     * Per-instance alpha for this frame: the model's own value, dimmed where the star is no
     * longer bound. In RENDER order, like `positions()` — `toRenderModel` sorts for painter's
     * order, so the k-th instance is `renderOrder[k]`, not the k-th particle.
     *
     * `boundFlags` is null with gravity off, and then every star is drawn at full alpha —
     * correct rather than convenient: without a potential being integrated there is no bound
     * set to belong to, which is the same reason the bound readouts blank there.
     */
    function alphas(): Float32Array {
      const out = new Float32Array(renderOrder.length);
      for (let k = 0; k < renderOrder.length; k++) {
        const i = renderOrder[k]!;
        const base = baseAlpha[k] ?? 1;
        out[k] = boundFlags && boundFlags[i] !== 1 ? base * ESCAPER_ALPHA : base;
      }
      return out;
    }

    function positions(): Float32Array {
      /* While scrubbing, the frame is drawn from the STORED snapshot. The integrator's own
         state is never written to — nothing is run backwards, and letting go returns to the
         live run because it was still there. */
      const src = scrubIndex !== null ? histPos[scrubIndex] : null;
      const st = run!.built.state;
      const out = new Float32Array(renderOrder.length * 3);
      for (let k = 0; k < renderOrder.length; k++) {
        const i = renderOrder[k]!;
        if (src) {
          out[3 * k] = src[3 * i]!;
          out[3 * k + 1] = src[3 * i + 1]!;
          out[3 * k + 2] = src[3 * i + 2]!;
        } else {
          out[3 * k] = st.pos[3 * i]!;
          out[3 * k + 1] = st.pos[3 * i + 1]!;
          out[3 * k + 2] = st.pos[3 * i + 2]!;
        }
      }
      return out;
    }

    function mountField() {
      if (!run) return;
      /* Synchronous now, so the async mount token that guarded reset / new draw /
         the N slider racing each other is gone with it. */
      if (points) {
        points.setModel(renderModel());
        frameR = run.built.viewPc;
        points.setFraming({ radiusPc: frameR });
        return;
      }
      /*
       * NO CAMERA DRIFT (the default, stated here because it is a claim). Every
       * pixel of motion on this page has to be attributable to gravity; a turning
       * camera would make "the massive stars sink" ambiguous with "the view
       * moved". Dragging still orbits.
       */
      /* `viewPc` is the scenario's own derived half-width — 4 r_h for the cluster.
         Using it as the opening frame keeps the choice where scenarios.ts puts it
         rather than re-deriving a first guess here. */
      frameR = run.built.viewPc;
      points = createClusterPoints(field, renderModel(), {
        drifting: false,
        /* pxPerPc is a placeholder until the device is up; the scale bar has to
           wait for it or it reports the placeholder's scale. */
        onReady: updateScaleBar,
      });
    }

    /*
     * Plot margins, sized for AXIS_FONT_LG (16px) rather than for the 9px these
     * were cut for — which never rendered anyway, so the old 34/18 was reserving
     * room for a label size that did not exist.
     *
     * The left margin is DERIVED, not chosen. Measured in the browser at 1440 px:
     * the rotated y-title's box ends 32 px in from the canvas's left edge, and the
     * widest tick label is 40 px ("2.00" at 16 px is 39; drawPower's "10" plus a
     * two-digit exponent is 40). Labels are right-aligned at `l - 8`, so clearing
     * the title by 4 px needs l >= 32 + 4 + 40 + 8 = 84. At 68 the log ticks on the
     * energy strip overlapped the title by 3 px.
     *
     * Bottom clears one line plus its gap.
     */
    const AX = { l: 84, r: 14, t: 12, b: 34 };
    /** Decades shown below a log axis's threshold — see the floor in drawSeries. */
    const LOG_DECADES = 5;
    /* How far above the data the limit may sit and still be drawn. Within a decade
       it is context; further off it is just a line at the top of an empty plot. */
    const LOG_NEAR_DECADES = 1;

    function drawSeries(
      cv: HTMLCanvasElement,
      series: { get: (p: (typeof hist)[number]) => number; colour: string; dash: number[] }[],
      opts: {
        zeroBase?: boolean;
        refOne?: boolean;
        fmt?: (v: number) => string;
        /**
         * A FIXED reference the axis must always contain, drawn as a line.
         *
         * Without one an autoscaled axis rescales to whatever the data does, so a
         * trace can never leave the frame — which made the energy strip's own note
         * ("if this walks steadily off the strip, stop believing the panel above
         * it") describe something the plot could not show. Pinning the axis to the
         * monitor's tolerance makes "off the strip" mean the same thing the
         * `healthy` verdict means, so the picture and the readout cannot disagree.
         */
        threshold?: { value: number; colour: string; label: string };
        /**
         * Plot log10(value) instead of the value.
         *
         * Energy drift spans DECADES — measured on this cluster it runs from below
         * 1e-7 early to a few times 1e-4 by ten crossing times — and the axis is
         * pinned to a 1e-3 trust limit. On a linear scale that put the entire trace
         * inside 4 pixels of a 276-pixel plot, hard against the bottom axis, which
         * reads exactly like a plot that has lost its history. It had not: 2,266 of
         * 2,428 columns carried trace pixels. The scale was wrong, not the record.
         *
         * `/dynamics-lab` plots the same quantity on fixed decades for the same
         * reason. Non-positive samples are dropped rather than clamped — log of a
         * zero drift is not a small number, it is not a number.
         */
        logY?: boolean;
        /** Pin the y-axis. A fraction plot must not autoscale — 0 and 1 are the
         *  meaningful ends, and a curve that never leaves 0.6-1.0 would otherwise
         *  be drawn as though it had swung across the whole panel. */
        fixed?: [number, number];
      } = {},
    ) {
      const { ctx, w, h } = fit(cv);
      ctx.clearRect(0, 0, w, h);
      const ink = css("--ink-faint", "#8b8f9a");
      const grid = css("--bg-hairline", "#2b2d38");
      const fmt = opts.fmt ?? ((v: number) => (Math.abs(v) >= 100 ? v.toFixed(0) : v.toFixed(2)));

      const tMax = hist.length > 1 ? hist[hist.length - 1].t || 1 : 1;
      /* The one place the log transform is applied. Everything downstream — the
         extent, the ticks, the threshold and the polyline — works in plotted
         units, so the two scales cannot disagree about where a value sits. */
      const toPlot = (v: number): number =>
        opts.logY ? (v > 0 ? Math.log10(v) : NaN) : v;
      /** Whether the limit line is close enough to the data to be worth drawing. */
      let showThreshold = false;
      let lo = Infinity;
      let hiV = -Infinity;
      for (const p of hist)
        for (const sr of series) {
          const v = toPlot(sr.get(p));
          if (Number.isFinite(v)) {
            lo = Math.min(lo, v);
            hiV = Math.max(hiV, v);
          }
        }
      if (opts.zeroBase && !opts.logY) lo = Math.min(lo, 0);
      if (opts.refOne) {
        lo = Math.min(lo, 1);
        hiV = Math.max(hiV, 1);
      }
      if (opts.threshold) {
        const tv = toPlot(opts.threshold.value);
        /*
         * THE LIMIT NO LONGER SETS THE SCALE.
         *
         * It used to force the axis to contain it, which meant a healthy run —
         * drift three or four decades under the line — was drawn as a flat trace
         * pinned to the bottom of a mostly-empty plot. The axis follows the DATA
         * so the trace is legible; the limit line is drawn only when it is close
         * enough to be worth seeing, and if the drift climbs toward it the axis
         * grows to meet it on its own.
         *
         * `showThreshold` is what the caller draws on, so a limit off the top of
         * the scale is simply not drawn rather than silently clamped to the edge,
         * where it would read as a limit the run was already touching.
         */
        if (Number.isFinite(tv) && tv <= hiV + LOG_NEAR_DECADES) {
          lo = Math.min(lo, tv);
          hiV = Math.max(hiV, tv);
          /*
           * FLOOR THE RANGE at a fixed number of decades below the limit.
           *
           * A log axis autoscaled to its data is at the mercy of its smallest
           * sample, and the first few drifts of a fresh run are near machine
           * precision — one of them would stretch the axis to a dozen decades and
           * squash the part anyone cares about into a sliver, which is the same
           * failure the linear axis had, mirrored. Anchoring the bottom to the
           * trust limit also makes the plot's shape comparable between runs
           * instead of rescaling under you every time.
           */
          if (opts.logY) lo = Math.max(lo, tv - LOG_DECADES);
          showThreshold = true;
        }
      }
      if (!Number.isFinite(lo) || !Number.isFinite(hiV)) {
        lo = 0;
        hiV = 1;
      }
      if (opts.fixed) {
        lo = opts.fixed[0];
        hiV = opts.fixed[1];
      }
      if (hiV === lo) hiV = lo + 1;
      if (!opts.fixed) {
        const pad = (hiV - lo) * 0.08;
        lo -= pad;
        hiV += pad;
      }

      const X = (t: number) => AX.l + (t / tMax) * (w - AX.l - AX.r);
      const Y = (v: number) => h - AX.b - ((v - lo) / (hiV - lo)) * (h - AX.t - AX.b);

      ctx.font = AXIS_FONT_LG;
      ctx.fillStyle = ink;
      ctx.strokeStyle = grid;
      ctx.lineWidth = 1;

      for (const v of niceTicks(lo, hiV)) {
        const y = Y(v);
        ctx.beginPath();
        ctx.moveTo(AX.l, y);
        ctx.lineTo(w - AX.r, y);
        ctx.stroke();
        ctx.textAlign = "right";
        ctx.textBaseline = "middle";
        if (opts.logY) {
          /* 10^x with a raised exponent — the same helper the H–R diagram and the
             IMF histogram label their decades with, but at THIS plot's font pair:
             left at the default these decades would draw 14px beside the linear
             plot's 16px. */
          drawPower(ctx, v, AX.l - 8, y, "right", ink, { base: AXIS_FONT_LG, sub: SUB_FONT_LG });
          ctx.font = AXIS_FONT_LG;
        } else {
          ctx.fillText(fmt(v), AX.l - 8, y);
        }
      }
      /* Vertical gridlines too, as the H–R diagram has: reading a time off a plot
         with horizontal rules only means tracking across bare space. */
      for (const t of niceTicks(0, tMax, 4)) {
        const x = X(t);
        ctx.beginPath();
        ctx.moveTo(x, AX.t);
        ctx.lineTo(x, h - AX.b);
        ctx.stroke();
        ctx.textAlign = "center";
        ctx.textBaseline = "top";
        ctx.fillText(t.toFixed(t < 10 ? 1 : 0), x, h - AX.b + 8);
      }
      /* Axis frame, matching renderHR's strokeRect — it separates the plotting
         area from the panel, which matters now that both panels have a filled
         background rather than the transparent one this file wrongly attributed
         to census. */
      ctx.strokeStyle = css("--border", "#2a2c31");
      ctx.strokeRect(AX.l, AX.t, w - AX.l - AX.r, h - AX.t - AX.b);
      ctx.strokeStyle = grid;

      if (opts.threshold && showThreshold) {
        const { value, colour, label } = opts.threshold;
        ctx.save();
        ctx.setLineDash([5, 4]);
        ctx.strokeStyle = colour;
        ctx.lineWidth = 1.25;
        ctx.beginPath();
        ctx.moveTo(AX.l, Y(toPlot(value)));
        ctx.lineTo(w - AX.r, Y(toPlot(value)));
        ctx.stroke();
        ctx.setLineDash([]);
        ctx.fillStyle = colour;
        ctx.textAlign = "left";
        ctx.textBaseline = "bottom";
        ctx.fillText(label, AX.l + 6, Y(toPlot(value)) - 3);
        ctx.restore();
        ctx.fillStyle = ink;
      }

      if (opts.refOne) {
        ctx.save();
        ctx.setLineDash([2, 3]);
        ctx.strokeStyle = ink;
        ctx.beginPath();
        ctx.moveTo(AX.l, Y(1));
        ctx.lineTo(w - AX.r, Y(1));
        ctx.stroke();
        ctx.restore();
      }

      /*
       * THE SCRUB CURSOR, on the time axis the tab shares with the timeline. Without it the
       * cluster shows one moment and the curves show the whole run, and a reader has no way to
       * see WHERE on them they are standing.
       */
      if (scrubIndex !== null && hist[scrubIndex]) {
        const xs = X(hist[scrubIndex]!.t);
        ctx.save();
        ctx.strokeStyle = css("--spec-teal-bright", "#4fd6c4");
        ctx.lineWidth = 1.25;
        ctx.setLineDash([3, 3]);
        ctx.beginPath();
        ctx.moveTo(xs, AX.t);
        ctx.lineTo(xs, h - AX.b);
        ctx.stroke();
        ctx.restore();
      }

      if (hist.length < 2) return;
      for (const sr of series) {
        ctx.beginPath();
        ctx.setLineDash(sr.dash);
        ctx.strokeStyle = sr.colour;
        ctx.lineWidth = 1.6;
        let started = false;
        for (const p of hist) {
          const raw = toPlot(sr.get(p));
          if (!Number.isFinite(raw)) continue;
          /* Clamped to the floor, not dropped: a sample quieter than the axis
             bottom is still a sample, and dropping it would break the trace into
             segments that look like missing history. */
          const v = Math.max(raw, lo);
          if (!started) {
            ctx.moveTo(X(p.t), Y(v));
            started = true;
          } else ctx.lineTo(X(p.t), Y(v));
        }
        ctx.stroke();
      }
      ctx.setLineDash([]);
    }

    /*
     * THE TAB'S OWN PLOT ONLY. The energy strip is drawn by its own function below, and the
     * two are called together by `drawPlots`.
     *
     * They were one function, and the receipt did not survive it. Each tab returns early —
     * `mf` and `lagr` do, because their plots are not time series — and the energy strip was
     * the last statement AFTER those returns, so it was simply never reached on those two
     * tabs. Measured in Chrome at 1512x857: with the clock running 6.7 -> 7.9 Myr, the energy
     * canvas's pixels were byte-identical the whole time on both `lagr` and `mf`, and started
     * changing again the instant the `radii` tab came back.
     *
     * The strip is the RECEIPT — this file's own note says it "stays OUTSIDE them
     * deliberately … it is the receipt that vouches for whichever tab you are on". On two of
     * four tabs it was vouching for a state the run had left, which is worse than showing
     * nothing: a stale trace still looks like a live one. The halt was never affected (it
     * fires from `sample()`), so what a reader lost was the visible approach to the limit.
     *
     * Splitting them is the fix rather than moving one line, because the shape that caused it
     * — "add a tab, copy the early return above" — will recur every time a tab is added, and
     * items on this page's own roadmap add more.
     */
    /*
     * THE BINARY'S ORBIT — a vs e, one dot per watch sample, coloured by segment.
     *
     * Its own renderer rather than `drawSeries`, because both axes are quantities: `drawSeries`
     * puts time across, and a track through (a, e) is the whole point here — hardening is a
     * LEFTWARD move, and that reads only if `a` is the x axis.
     *
     * NOTHING IS SMOOTHED, and that is a decision. `a` and `e` are instantaneous, from a relative
     * energy the rest of the cluster perturbs and the softening modifies, so one steady orbit
     * still draws a cloud — measured, a spans a factor of 1.7 to 2.9 within a single pair's own
     * track. A fitted line through that would assert a Keplerian orbit the pair is not on.
     */
    const drawPairTrack = () => {
      const { ctx, w, h } = fit(radiiCv);
      ctx.clearRect(0, 0, w, h);
      const ink = css("--ink-faint", "#8b8f9a");
      const grid = css("--bg-hairline", "#2b2d38");
      const rose = css("--spec-rose-bright", "#e58fb4");
      const amber = css("--spec-amber", "#fbbf24");
      if (pairTrack.length < 1) return;

      /* a spans decades over a hardening binary's life, so the x axis is log. e does not — it is
         bounded by construction — so it is pinned to 0..1 and stays comparable between runs. */
      const as = pairTrack.map((p) => p.a).filter((v) => v > 0);
      if (!as.length) return;
      const lx0 = Math.log10(Math.min(...as));
      const lx1 = Math.log10(Math.max(...as));
      /* A fresh track is one dot, so the extent is a point; give it a decade to live in rather
         than dividing by zero. */
      const padX = lx1 - lx0 < 0.5 ? (0.5 - (lx1 - lx0)) / 2 : (lx1 - lx0) * 0.08;
      const [xLo, xHi] = [lx0 - padX, lx1 + padX];
      const X = (a: number) => AX.l + ((Math.log10(a) - xLo) / (xHi - xLo)) * (w - AX.l - AX.r);
      const Y = (e: number) => h - AX.b - e * (h - AX.t - AX.b);

      ctx.font = AXIS_FONT_LG;
      ctx.strokeStyle = grid;
      ctx.lineWidth = 1;
      ctx.fillStyle = ink;
      for (const e of [0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1]) {
        const y = Y(e);
        ctx.beginPath();
        ctx.moveTo(AX.l, y);
        ctx.lineTo(w - AX.r, y);
        ctx.stroke();
        ctx.textAlign = "right";
        ctx.textBaseline = "middle";
        ctx.fillText(e.toFixed(2), AX.l - 8, y);
      }
      for (let n = Math.ceil(xLo); n <= Math.floor(xHi); n++) {
        const x = X(10 ** n);
        ctx.beginPath();
        ctx.moveTo(x, AX.t);
        ctx.lineTo(x, h - AX.b);
        ctx.stroke();
        drawPower(ctx, n, x, h - AX.b + 14, "center", ink, { base: AXIS_FONT_LG, sub: SUB_FONT_LG });
        ctx.font = AXIS_FONT_LG;
      }
      ctx.strokeStyle = css("--border", "#2a2c31");
      ctx.strokeRect(AX.l, AX.t, w - AX.l - AX.r, h - AX.t - AX.b);

      /* Segment 1 is the pair as it formed; every later segment follows an exchange. Two
         colours, not a ramp — the question is "is this still the same binary", which is
         categorical. */
      const first = pairTrack[0]!.seg;
      for (const p of pairTrack) {
        if (!(p.a > 0)) continue;
        ctx.fillStyle = p.seg === first ? rose : amber;
        ctx.globalAlpha = 0.55;
        ctx.beginPath();
        ctx.arc(X(p.a), Y(p.e), 2.2, 0, Math.PI * 2);
        ctx.fill();
      }
      /* The latest sample, solid and larger — without it a reader cannot tell which end of the
         cloud is now, and "hardening moves it left" is unreadable. */
      const last = pairTrack[pairTrack.length - 1]!;
      if (last.a > 0) {
        ctx.globalAlpha = 1;
        ctx.fillStyle = last.seg === first ? rose : amber;
        ctx.beginPath();
        ctx.arc(X(last.a), Y(last.e), 4.2, 0, Math.PI * 2);
        ctx.fill();
      }
      ctx.globalAlpha = 1;
    };

    const drawTabPlot = () => {
      const rose = css("--spec-rose-bright", "#e58fb4");
      const teal = css("--spec-teal-bright", "#4fd6c4");
      if (plotTab === "binary") {
        drawPairTrack();
        return;
      }
      if (plotTab === "mf") {
        /*
         * BORN under, STILL BOUND over — a subset drawn on top of the set, so the exposed
         * remainder of each bar is what has left. The evolution here is in the DIFFERENCE
         * between two samples, which is why this tab is not a time series and why the axis
         * titles swap with it.
         */
        const { ctx, w, h } = fit(radiiCv);
        const identity = run?.built.identity;
        if (!bornIMF || !identity) {
          ctx.clearRect(0, 0, w, h);
          return;
        }
        const flags = boundFlags;
        if (!flags) {
          mfCounts = null;
        } else if (mfStale || mfCounts === null) {
          const kept = latentStars().filter((star) => flags[star.id] === 1);
          mfCounts = toIMFHistogram(kept, identity).bins.map((b) => b.count);
          mfStale = false;
          setStat("legend-kept", String(kept.length));
        }
        if (!flags) setStat("legend-kept", "—");
        const overlay = mfCounts ? { counts: mfCounts, colour: teal } : undefined;
        renderHistogram(ctx, bornIMF, w, h, {
          /* Counts per dex, not dN/dM: the question here is "how many are left", and a
             division by bin width puts the answer in units nobody is counting in. */
          form: "perDex",
          /* LINEAR, unlike census: a log axis compresses the very depletion this tab is
             for — see the note on `scale` in viz/histogram.ts. */
          scale: "linear",
          overlay,
          font: AXIS_FONT_LG,
          margin: { left: AX.l, right: AX.r, top: AX.t, bottom: AX.b },
          colors: {
            bar: css("--ink-faint", "#8b8f9a"),
            line: rose,
            axis: css("--border", "#2a2c31"),
            grid: css("--bg-hairline", "#2b2d38"),
            text: css("--ink-faint", "#8b8f9a"),
          },
        });
        return;
      }
      if (plotTab === "lagr") {
        /* Absolute parsecs, zero-based: the story is that the outer radius runs away from the
           inner one, and a floating baseline would hide how far apart they get. */
        drawSeries(
          radiiCv,
          [
            { get: (p) => p.l20, colour: rose, dash: [] },
            { get: (p) => p.l50, colour: teal, dash: [4, 3] },
            { get: (p) => p.l90, colour: css("--spec-amber", "#fbbf24"), dash: [] },
          ],
          { zeroBase: true },
        );
        return;
      }
      if (plotTab === "bound") {
        /* Fractions, so the axis is PINNED to 0..1. Autoscaled, a run that never
           left 0.6-1.0 would be drawn as though it had swung across the whole
           panel, and the one number that matters — how far from 1 — would be
           unreadable. */
        drawSeries(
          radiiCv,
          [
            { get: (p) => p.bm, colour: rose, dash: [] },
            { get: (p) => p.bn, colour: teal, dash: [4, 3] },
          ],
          /*
           * PINNED, BUT NOT TO ZERO. The axis must not autoscale — 1 is the meaningful top and
           * a curve that never leaves 0.7-1.0 would otherwise be drawn as though it had swung
           * across the whole panel. But [0, 1] spent about seventy percent of the height on
           * fractions this cluster never reaches: measured over twenty crossing times the two
           * curves occupy 0.72 to 0.97. The floor is 0.4 rather than 0.7 so a long run has
           * somewhere to go — the lowest bound fraction on record here is near 0.49.
           */
          { fixed: [0.4, 1], fmt: (v) => v.toFixed(1) },
        );
      } else {
        drawSeries(
          radiiCv,
          [
            { get: (p) => p.hi, colour: rose, dash: [] },
            { get: (p) => p.lo, colour: teal, dash: [4, 3] },
          ],
          { refOne: true },
        );
      }
    };

    /* Drawn on EVERY tab, which is the whole point of it — see the note on `drawTabPlot`. */
    const drawEnergyStrip = () => {
      /* The tolerance comes from the MONITOR, not from a number typed here — it is
         the same value `healthy` latches on, so the line a reader sees and the
         verdict the page reports cannot drift apart. */
      const tol = run?.monitor.energyTolerance;
      drawSeries(energyCv, [{ get: (p) => p.e, colour: css("--spec-amber", "#fbbf24"), dash: [] }], {
        logY: true,
        ...(tol
          ? { threshold: { value: tol, colour: css("--spec-rose-bright", "#e58fb4"), label: "trust limit" } }
          : {}),
      });
    };

    const drawPlots = () => {
      drawTabPlot();
      drawEnergyStrip();
    };

    /*
     * The stored offsets are in the pair's COM frame; placing them needs the CURRENT centre of
     * mass, because that point is itself moving through the cluster. Rebuilt per frame, which
     * is a few hundred adds — against the alternative of storing world positions, where the
     * orbit would be smeared along the binary's own path through the cluster and invisible.
     */
    const drawPairTrail = (): void => {
      if (!followPair || !pair || !run || pairTrail.length < 6) {
        points?.setTrail(null);
        return;
      }
      const st2 = run.built.state;
      const mi = st2.mass[pair.i]!;
      const mj = st2.mass[pair.j]!;
      const mT = mi + mj;
      const out = new Float32Array(pairTrail.length);
      for (let k = 0; k < pairTrail.length; k += 3) {
        for (let c = 0; c < 3; c++) {
          const com = (st2.pos[pair.i * 3 + c]! * mi + st2.pos[pair.j * 3 + c]! * mj) / mT;
          out[k + c] = pairTrail[k + c]! + com;
        }
      }
      points?.setTrail(out, [0.9, 0.56, 0.71]); // --spec-rose-bright, as the binary tab uses
    };

    const redraw = () => {
      syncScrubRange();
      points?.setPositions(positions());
      points?.setAlpha(alphas());
      drawPairTrail();
      drawPlots();
      updateScaleBar(); // zoom and resize both change it without a physics step
    };

    /* ── loop ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
    /*
     * Cancelled on pause, when the page is hidden, and when the engine scrolls
     * out of view. The dynamics LAB re-schedules unconditionally and never
     * stops — tolerable on an unlinked development surface, not on a page a
     * reader leaves open in a background tab.
     */
    let raf = 0;
    let onScreen = true;

    /*
     * FREE STREAMING — what "no gravity" actually looks like.
     *
     * This used to hold the cluster STATIC, on the reasoning that a zero-force
     * model would have to be invented. It does not: removing the mutual force
     * leaves `x += v dt`, which is exact, and the stars already carry the
     * velocities the initial conditions drew for them. So a cluster without
     * gravity is not a still image — it disperses in straight lines, because
     * nothing is holding it together.
     *
     * That mistake is what made the toggle useless. "Static" is indistinguishable
     * from "paused", so the page had two controls doing one thing, and the button
     * marked run had to override the toggle to do anything at all. The physical
     * answer removes the redundancy instead of papering over it — and it is the
     * page's own title: take the coupling away and there is no cluster.
     */
    function drift(dt: number) {
      const st = run!.built.state;
      for (let i = 0; i < st.n * 3; i++) st.pos[i] += st.vel[i] * dt;
    }

    /* ── the banner ────────────────────────────────────────────── */
    const alertEl = $<HTMLElement>("[data-alert]");
    const alertTitle = $<HTMLElement>("[data-alert-title]");
    const alertBody = $<HTMLElement>("[data-alert-body]");
    const zoomPairBtn = $<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-zoom-pair]");

    /*
     * The binary readout, driven by the SAME `pair` object the banner is. One source, so the
     * chip and the row cannot report different orbits — which they would, sooner or later, if
     * each recomputed the pair on its own schedule.
     */
    const pairStats = $<HTMLElement>("[data-pair-stats]");
    function showPairStats(p: NonNullable<ReturnType<typeof pairResolution>>): void {
      setStat("pair-m", `${p.masses[0].toFixed(0)}+${p.masses[1].toFixed(0)}`);
      setStat("pair-a", p.semiMajorAxis.toExponential(2));
      setStat("pair-e", p.eccentricity.toFixed(3));
      setStat("pair-p", p.period.toExponential(2));
      setStat("pair-hard", p.hardness.toFixed(0));
      /* "—" rather than "NaN" when the scheme sizes its own step — see `resolvedStep`. */
      setStat("pair-steps", Number.isFinite(p.stepsPerOrbit) ? p.stepsPerOrbit.toFixed(0) : "—");
      pairStats.hidden = false;
    }
    function hidePairStats(): void {
      pairStats.hidden = true;
    }

    /*
     * The binary tab exists exactly while the binary does.
     *
     * If it is the ACTIVE tab when the pair goes, the page falls back rather than leaving a
     * selected tab drawing nothing — a blank panel with a lit tab reads as a broken plot, and
     * the reader has no way to know the binary was simply broken up.
     */
    const pairTabBtn = $<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-pair-tab]");
    const syncPairTab = (): void => {
      pairTabBtn.hidden = !pairConfirmed;
      if (!pairConfirmed && plotTab === "binary") {
        selectTab("radii");
        syncUrl();
      }
    };

    function showAlert(title: string, body: string, canZoom: boolean): void {
      alertTitle.textContent = title;
      /* The bodies carry typeset symbols from the library above, so this is innerHTML — the
         content is this file's own strings, never a reader's. */
      alertBody.innerHTML = body;
      zoomPairBtn.hidden = !canZoom || !pair;
      alertEl.hidden = false;
    }
    function hideAlert(): void {
      alertEl.hidden = true;
    }

    /* ── the binary watch ──────────────────────────────────────── */
    /*
     * Thresholds in STEPS PER ORBIT of the tightest bound pair, which is the quantity that
     * predicts the energy error — see `core/dynamics/binaries.ts` for the measurements the
     * numbers come from. Below ~200 the drift on seed 2028 had already climbed three orders;
     * by 76 it was pinned near the trust limit.
     *
     * WATCH is set well above the damage (400) so the reader is told BEFORE the receipt goes
     * bad rather than after. HALT is far below it (30), so stopping stays rare and means the
     * orbit is genuinely unresolvable rather than merely coarse.
     */
    const PAIR_WATCH_STEPS = 400;
    const PAIR_HALT_STEPS = 30;
    /* O(N^2) — about one diagnostics pass at N = 800 — so it is not run every frame. */
    const PAIR_EVERY = 8;
    /*
     * A BINARY IS DEFINED BY PERSISTING, and without this the banner lies.
     *
     * The threshold alone fires on any close passage: measured live on seed 2028 it announced
     * "a hard binary has formed" at t/t_cr = 5.3 for a 2 + 38 Msun pair at hardness 15 — a
     * flyby, gone moments later. The actual binary on that seed is 38.5 + 29.9 Msun and forms
     * around t/t_cr = 15, where it then STAYS for the rest of the run.
     *
     * So the same two stars must hold the condition for half a crossing time before the page
     * says anything. That is a physical criterion rather than a debounce: an unbound pair
     * separates on roughly its own encounter timescale, so surviving half a crossing time
     * together is what distinguishes a bound pair from two stars passing.
     */
    const PAIR_PERSIST_TCROSS = 0.5;
    /*
     * Entering and leaving are DIFFERENT questions, and running both off the watch threshold is
     * what made the binary flicker. The rules, and the runs that measured them, are in
     * `core/dynamics/pairWatch` — restating them here is how the two would drift apart.
     */
    const PAIR_RELEASE_HARDNESS = 1;
    let pairFrame = 0;
    let pair: ReturnType<typeof pairResolution> = null;
    /*
     * THE STATE MACHINE IS `core/dynamics/pairWatch`, not this file.
     *
     * Enter, stay, release and exchange are four distinct physical claims, two of which were
     * measured wrong here before they were extractable — and none of which could be tested at
     * all while they lived in a `<script>` block. The module carries the rules and the
     * measurements behind them; this file just reports what it says.
     */
    const pairWatch = createPairWatch({
      watchSteps: PAIR_WATCH_STEPS,
      persistTcross: PAIR_PERSIST_TCROSS,
      releaseHardness: PAIR_RELEASE_HARDNESS,
    });
    /** Mirrors `pairWatch.confirmed`; read by the banner, the slow-down, the tab and the halt. */
    let pairConfirmed = false;
    /**
     * The confirmed pair's own indices, which OUTLIVE an exchange.
     *
     * An exchange swaps one companion and keeps the other — measured on three of four seeds,
     * at t/t_cr 21.9, 18.7, and 19.8 and 21.8. Keyed on the raw `i:j` string that reads as a
     * brand-new pair, so the persistence clock restarted and the binary vanished from the page
     * for ~1 crossing time at the exact moment the most interesting thing happened to it.
     *
     * Sharing one star is a CONTINUATION of the same binary, so the clock is not restarted and
     * the track below keeps going — with the exchange recorded rather than papered over.
     */

    /**
     * The confirmed binary's orbit, sampled at the watch cadence — the `a` vs `e` tab's data.
     *
     * `seg` is what lets an exchange read as a jump rather than as a smear: the track changes
     * colour where the companion changed, so a reader sees that the point moved because the
     * BINARY changed, not because the orbit drifted there.
     */
    let pairTrack: { t: number; a: number; e: number; hard: number; seg: number }[] = [];
    const MAX_PAIR_SAMPLES = 600;
    /*
     * The binary is over: clear everything keyed to it, in one place.
     *
     * Three call sites had grown their own partial versions of this, and the one that mattered
     * — the adaptive-scheme branch — cleared the clock but not `pairMembers`, so a later pair
     * sharing an index with a long-dead one would have counted as its continuation.
     */
    const releasePair = (): void => {
      pairWatch.reset();
      pairConfirmed = false;
      pairTrack = [];
    };
    /** Whether the frame is TRACKING the binary rather than the cluster. See sample(). */
    let followPair = false;
    /*
     * THE PAIR'S ORBIT, SAMPLED EVERY INTEGRATOR STEP.
     *
     * `pairResolution` is O(N^2) and runs every eighth frame, which is fine for a readout and
     * useless for a curve: at the point the page is about, one orbit takes tens of steps, so a
     * trail sampled at that cadence would be three or four points and would draw a triangle
     * where the physics has an ellipse. But `i` and `j` are already known between searches, so
     * reading those two stars' positions costs six array loads and can happen every step.
     *
     * THIS IS A TRAJECTORY PLOT, NOT A DECORATION, and the distinction has a test: every point
     * is a state the integrator actually produced. Nothing is interpolated between samples —
     * which is the tempting shortcut when an orbit is short, and is exactly where this would
     * stop being the model's own history and start being a drawing of what an orbit ought to
     * look like.
     *
     * Stored in the pair's CENTRE-OF-MASS FRAME, because the binary is also orbiting in the
     * cluster and recoiling: in world coordinates the samples smear along that path and no
     * ellipse is visible. The frame change is stated on the page rather than assumed.
     */
    let pairTrail: number[] = [];
    const MAX_TRAIL_SAMPLES = 2400;
    let halted = false;

    /*
     * THE STEP IS NOT SHORTENED, the CLOCK IS SLOWED, and the difference is the whole point.
     *
     * Shrinking dt mid-run is the instinct, and for FSI4 it is exactly wrong: a symplectic
     * map's bounded error comes from conserving one shadow Hamiltonian H~ = H + O(h^4), and
     * changing h mid-run switches to a different H~ whose mismatch accumulates. It would
     * trade a visible, bounded error for an invisible, secular one.
     *
     * So the accuracy is left alone and the SUBSTEP COUNT falls instead: fewer steps of the
     * same size per frame, so less model time passes per second and the reader can watch the
     * pair rather than the run racing past it. The real answer to a hardening binary is a
     * scheme that adapts legitimately — `logh.ts` — not a fudge here.
     */
    function substepsNow(): number {
      /* Gated on `pairConfirmed`, not on the raw threshold: otherwise every close passage
         would jolt the clock for a few frames, which reads as stuttering rather than as the
         deliberate slow-down it is meant to be. */
      if (!pairConfirmed || !pair) return SUBSTEPS;
      /* No step size, no slow-down: the criterion is a statement about a fixed step. */
      if (!Number.isFinite(pair.stepsPerOrbit) || pair.stepsPerOrbit >= PAIR_WATCH_STEPS) return SUBSTEPS;
      const frac = Math.max(0.125, pair.stepsPerOrbit / PAIR_WATCH_STEPS);
      return Math.max(1, Math.round(SUBSTEPS * frac));
    }

    function halt(title: string, body: string): void {
      halted = true;
      playing = false;
      stop();
      syncPlay();
      showAlert(title, body, false);
    }

    /*
     * THE STEP THE INTEGRATOR ACTUALLY TAKES [Myr] — not the frame's dt. Null when the
     * scheme sizes its own step and does not report it.
     *
     * `steps/orbit` was computed from the FRAME dt, and it is wrong there in two ways:
     *
     *  - THE FRAME dt IS NOT THE STEP. Every fixed-step scheme subdivides it internally
     *    (`nSub = ceil(dt / maxStep)` in fsi4.ts), so a readout taken from the frame's dt
     *    under-reports the resolution by that factor and every threshold keyed on it fires
     *    early. This bit hardest under the old speed control, where the frame dt was a
     *    multiple of maxStep; `?autostep` reaches the same place from the other direction by
     *    shortening dt below it.
     *
     *  - THE ADAPTIVE SCHEMES DO NOT HAVE ONE FIXED STEP AT ALL. LogH's physical step floats
     *    as h_s/(-U) and shrinks exactly where a binary tightens — which is the entire reason
     *    to use it — so `period / frameDt` describes a step it is not taking. It reports
     *    `lastPhysicalStep`, so ask it. Hermite's adaptive arm sizes each sub-step from the
     *    Aarseth criterion and reports nothing, so there is no honest number to print: it
     *    gets null, and every claim keyed on steps-per-orbit is withheld rather than guessed.
     *
     * That last case matters most: the halt below ENDS THE RUN and names a figure while doing
     * it. Ending a LogH run for a resolution failure it is not having — on a page whose own
     * copy says LogH "fixes exactly that" — is the worst version of this bug.
     */
    function resolvedStep(frameDt: number): number | null {
      const maxStep = run!.built.timeUnit / stepDivisor;
      if (scheme === "logh") {
        const h = (run!.integrator as { lastPhysicalStep?: number }).lastPhysicalStep;
        return typeof h === "number" && h > 0 ? h : null;
      }
      /* Hermite is only ever built adaptive here (`adaptive: scheme === "hermite"`). */
      if (scheme === "hermite") return null;
      return frameDt / Math.max(1, Math.ceil(frameDt / maxStep));
    }

    function watchBinary(dt: number): void {
      if (++pairFrame % PAIR_EVERY !== 0) return;
      /* The softening comes from the BUILD, never recomputed here — the pair this reports
         has to be the pair the force model actually feels, and a second copy of
         `softeningForCluster` in this file is how the two would drift apart. */
      const h = resolvedStep(dt);
      if (h === null) {
        /* Still find the pair — a, e, P and hardness are properties of the ORBIT and stay
           true whatever the integrator does. Only the steps-per-orbit claim is withheld. */
        const base = hardestBoundPair(run!.built.state, run!.built.softeningPc);
        pair = base ? { ...base, stepsPerOrbit: NaN } : null;
      } else {
        pair = pairResolution(run!.built.state, run!.built.softeningPc, h);
      }
      if (!pair) return;
      /* Every threshold below is a statement about a step size. Without one there is nothing
         to compare, so the watch, the slow-down and the halt all stand down. The energy limit
         still guards the run, and Hermite's own arm throws with its own message if the pair
         hardens past what it can resolve. */
      if (!Number.isFinite(pair.stepsPerOrbit)) {
        const wasOn = pairConfirmed;
        releasePair();
        if (wasOn) syncPlay();
        if (!halted) {
          hideAlert();
          hidePairStats();
        }
        return;
      }

      /* Enter, stay, release, exchange — all four in `pairWatch`, tested there. */
      const watch = pairWatch.update(pair, tSim, run!.built.timeUnit);
      pairConfirmed = watch.confirmed;
      if (!watch.confirmed) pairTrack = [];
      const persisted = pairConfirmed;
      /* One sample per watch, which is every PAIR_EVERY frames — the same cadence the readouts
         update at, so the track cannot claim resolution the numbers beside it do not have. */
      if (pairConfirmed) {
        pairTrack.push({
          t: tSim,
          a: pair.semiMajorAxis,
          e: pair.eccentricity,
          hard: pair.hardness,
          seg: watch.segment,
        });
        /* HALVED, never shifted. `shift()` would drop the oldest samples, and the oldest
           samples are the pair AT FORMATION — the wide, soft end of the track that makes the
           hardening legible as a direction. Halving keeps the whole span and thins it. */
        if (pairTrack.length > MAX_PAIR_SAMPLES)
          pairTrack = pairTrack.filter((_, i) => i % 2 === 0);
      }
      if (pairConfirmed && pair) {
        setStatHTML(
          "legend-pair",
          `${pair.masses[0].toFixed(0)}+${pair.masses[1].toFixed(0)} ${tex("msun")}`,
        );
        setStatHTML(
          "legend-pairnow",
          `${tex("a")} = ${pair.semiMajorAxis.toExponential(1)} ${tex("pc")} · ` +
            `${tex("ecc")} = ${pair.eccentricity.toFixed(2)}`,
        );
      }
      /* The status line under the canvas reports the slow-down, and it is only rewritten by
         syncPlay — so a transition here has to poke it, or the line goes on saying "every star
         pulls on every other" while the clock is visibly crawling. */
      if (watch.changed) {
        syncPlay();
        syncPairTab();
      }

      /*
       * `persisted` GATES THE HALT TOO, and it did not.
       *
       * The banner has required half a crossing time since it was written, for a reason
       * spelled out above: the raw threshold fires on any close passage. Measured live on
       * seed 2028 it announced a hard binary at t/t_cr = 5.3 for a 2 + 38 Msun pair — a
       * flyby, gone moments later.
       *
       * The HALT was left on the bare threshold, which is the worse place for it: two stars
       * momentarily bound at a small separation have a small `a` and a short period, so a
       * single passage can read below thirty steps per orbit and END THE RUN, asserting an
       * unresolvable binary that does not exist. The banner merely appears and leaves; the
       * halt cannot be undone without a reset.
       *
       * Same criterion, same reason. The energy limit still guards the run in the meantime,
       * and it is the one that catches a pair which really is destroying the integration.
       */
      if (pair.stepsPerOrbit < PAIR_HALT_STEPS && persisted) {
        /* Longer than the watch banner, and that is proportionate: the run has STOPPED, and a
           reader is owed the reason on the spot rather than in a paragraph below the fold. */
        halt(
          "Stopped — binary unresolvable",
          `${pair.masses[0].toFixed(0)}+${pair.masses[1].toFixed(0)} ${tex("msun")} at ` +
            `${tex("a")} = ${pair.semiMajorAxis.toExponential(1)} ${tex("pc")} is down to ` +
            `${pair.stepsPerOrbit.toFixed(0)} steps/orbit. Below this the arithmetic no longer ` +
            `describes the orbit, so the run stops rather than showing you a cluster it has ` +
            `stopped solving. Reset or draw again.`,
        );
        showPairStats(pair); // the stop's own explanation — keep the figures on screen
        return;
      }
      /*
       * KEYED ON THE BINARY, NOT ON THE THRESHOLD — and this is the half of the flicker fix
       * that was missed the first time.
       *
       * `pairWatch` was given a release rule that holds a confirmed pair across a
       * steps-per-orbit excursion, because `a` oscillates and spo goes as a^3/2. But the
       * BANNER kept its own `pair.stepsPerOrbit < PAIR_WATCH_STEPS` test, so it went on
       * hiding and reappearing on exactly the excursions the watch had just been taught to
       * ignore — measured, 29 crossings of the 400 mark in 12 crossing times on seed 2028.
       * Anna saw it flickering; the confirmation underneath it was perfectly stable.
       *
       * `persisted` IS the question the banner asks ("is there a hard binary?"), so it is the
       * only condition. The steps-per-orbit figure still moves inside the banner, which is a
       * readout changing rather than a banner appearing.
       */
      if (persisted) {
        /*
         * ONE LINE OF NUMBERS. The first version put the whole explanation here and covered
         * about 40% of the cluster with text — over the one thing the panel exists to show.
         * The reasoning lives in the energy strip's "?" and in the page body; the banner's
         * job is to say THAT it happened, with the figures, and offer to show you.
         */
        /*
         * Trimmed to hold ONE line in the 500 px panel, and the trimming was measured rather
         * than guessed: at --step--2 (14.96 px here) the full string needed 485 px of text
         * plus an 86 px button against 484 px available, so it wrapped to three lines.
         *
         * Hardness went to the energy strip's "?" — steps-per-orbit is the number that
         * explains what the trace below is DOING — and "clock slowed" went to the status line
         * under the canvas, which is where every other phrase about what the run is currently
         * doing already lives.
         */
        showAlert(
          "Hard binary",
          `${pair.masses[0].toFixed(0)}+${pair.masses[1].toFixed(0)} ${tex("msun")} · ` +
            `${pair.stepsPerOrbit.toFixed(0)} steps/orbit`,
          true,
        );
        showPairStats(pair);
      } else if (!halted) {
        hideAlert();
        hidePairStats();
      }
    }

    function frame() {
      raf = 0;
      if (!run || !playing) return;
      /* The guard only ever shortens, so this can fall mid-run but never climb back. */
      const dt =
        (run.built.timeUnit / stepDivisor) * (stepGuard?.scale ?? 1);
      /*
       * WRAPPED, because a throw here does not fail loudly — it fails INVISIBLY.
       *
       * An exception escaping a requestAnimationFrame callback skips the re-schedule at the
       * bottom, so the loop simply stops: the cluster freezes, the button still reads
       * "pause", and nothing on the page says why. Hermite's adaptive arm throws by design
       * when a pair hardens past what it can resolve — its own message says so — and that
       * arm is reachable from the integrator toggle, so this is a live path and not a
       * hypothetical one.
       */
      try {
        const subs = substepsNow();
        for (let k = 0; k < subs; k++) {
          if (gravity) {
            run.integrator.step(dt);
            /* Every step, and only while the reader is looking at the pair — see pairTrail. */
            if (followPair && pair) {
              const st2 = run.built.state;
              const i = pair.i;
              const j = pair.j;
              const mi = st2.mass[i]!;
              const mj = st2.mass[j]!;
              const mT = mi + mj;
              /* The LIGHTER star's offset from the pair's centre of mass: the ellipse that
                 actually passes through a star the reader can see moving. */
              const light = mi <= mj ? i : j;
              for (let c = 0; c < 3; c++) {
                const com = (st2.pos[i * 3 + c]! * mi + st2.pos[j * 3 + c]! * mj) / mT;
                pairTrail.push(st2.pos[light * 3 + c]! - com);
              }
              if (pairTrail.length > MAX_TRAIL_SAMPLES * 3)
                pairTrail = pairTrail.slice(pairTrail.length - MAX_TRAIL_SAMPLES * 3);
            }
            /*
             * THE MONITOR IS POLLED EVERY STEP, NOT ONCE A FRAME — AND THAT IS THE
             * DIFFERENCE BETWEEN NOTICING THE BREACH AND NOT.
             *
             * This ran in `sample()`, once per frame, while a frame advances SUBSTEPS
             * (8) steps. A symplectic scheme's energy error is not a slow drift: it
             * arrives as narrow spikes at the periapsis of the hard binary, and a
             * spike is over inside one step. Sampling every eighth step aliases them
             * away, so the run sailed past the trust limit it claims to enforce.
             *
             * Measured on this cluster at N = 400, worst |dE/E| over 25 crossing
             * times as a function of the sampling stride:
             *
             *   stride   seed 2026   seed 2028   seed 2029
             *        1    1.02e-4     2.22e-4     9.02e-4   <- the truth
             *        8    8.95e-5     8.44e-5     8.99e-4   <- what this page saw
             *     1024    8.88e-8     4.13e-5     5.08e-4
             *
             * At stride 8 the DEFAULT draw peaks at 8.95e-5 and never trips the 1e-4
             * limit, while the trajectory it is drawing actually crosses it at
             * t/t_cr = 19.2. Seed 2028 crosses at 7.25 and was likewise never caught.
             * The halt fired on one of three draws, ten crossing times late.
             *
             * So the limit is now tested against every state the integrator produces.
             * The cost is one O(N^2) potential per step on top of the accelerations
             * and force gradient FSI4 already needs — the expensive per-frame work
             * (boundness, the Lagrangian sorts, the draw) stays once per frame.
             */
            const drift = run.monitor.sample().energyDrift;
            worstSinceStore = Math.max(worstSinceStore, drift);
            /*
             * EVERY STEP, not once a frame — the same reason the monitor itself moved here. The
             * error arrives as narrow spikes at the binary's periapsis, and sampling every
             * eighth step aliased them away once already.
             */
            if (stepGuard) {
              const ev = stepGuard.update(drift, run.integrator.t);
              if (ev) syncStepNote();
            }
          } else {
            drift(dt);
            tSim += dt;
          }
        }
        /*
         * WITH GRAVITY ON, THE INTEGRATOR'S CLOCK IS THE CLOCK — read, not accumulated.
         *
         * This used to be `tSim += dt` on both paths, which is exact for a fixed-step scheme
         * and WRONG for LogH: it takes whole steps in fictitious time and stops on the first
         * one past the target, deliberately, because truncating the last step would forfeit
         * the symplecticity that is the entire reason to use it. Its `t` is therefore slightly
         * ahead of the requested dt, and an accumulator would drift away from it — the readout
         * and the energy history would end up describing a different time from the state.
         *
         * Free streaming keeps its own accumulator because that path never touches the
         * integrator. The two can only disagree if gravity changes mid-run, and it cannot:
         * `selectGravity` rebuilds, resetting both to zero together.
         */
        if (gravity) tSim = run.integrator.t;
        if (gravity) watchBinary(dt);
      } catch (err) {
        halt(
          "The integrator stopped",
          err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err),
        );
        return;
      }
      sample();
      redraw();
      if (playing && onScreen && !document.hidden) raf = requestAnimationFrame(frame);
    }

    const start = () => {
      /* `halted` gates here rather than only at the button, so neither the visibility handler
         nor the intersection observer can quietly restart a run the model has given up on. */
      if (halted) return;
      if (!raf && playing && onScreen && !document.hidden) raf = requestAnimationFrame(frame);
    };
    const stop = () => {
      if (raf) cancelAnimationFrame(raf);
      raf = 0;
    };

    document.addEventListener("visibilitychange", () => (document.hidden ? stop() : start()));
    new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {
      onScreen = entries[0]?.isIntersecting ?? true;
      onScreen ? start() : stop();
    }).observe(root);

    /* ── wiring ────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
    /* The strip says WHICH scheme drew the trace, always — a bounded-error claim
       that silently applies to a secular integrator is the failure mode the whole
       panel exists to prevent. */
    const syncScheme = () => {
      const el = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-schemename]");
      /* Each name carries the claim the strip below is entitled to make, and they differ:
         "bounded" is only true of a symplectic map, and LogH is the one that is BOTH
         symplectic and adaptive. Labelling them alike would let the panel's argument survive
         a switch that invalidates it. */
      if (el)
        el.textContent =
          scheme === "fsi4"
            ? "FSI4 · bounded"
            : scheme === "logh"
              ? "LogH · bounded, adaptive"
              : "Hermite · secular";
    };

    /*
     * ANY DEPARTURE FROM THIS PAGE'S OWN SETTINGS ANNOUNCES ITSELF.
     *
     * The paragraph under the figure states ε = 0.1 r_h N^(−1/3) and a fixed step, and the
     * energy strip's argument rests on the step being fixed. A knob that changed either
     * silently would leave both asserting something untrue of the run on screen — which is the
     * class of quiet untruth this component is built against. So each override names the
     * default it replaced, and a step the guard has shortened says when and why.
     */
    const syncStepNote = (): void => {
      const el = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-epsnote]");
      if (!el || !run) return;
      const parts: string[] = [];
      if (softeningFraction !== SOFTENING_FRACTION)
        parts.push(
          `${tex("eps")} = ${softeningFraction} ${tex("epslaw")} = ` +
            `${run.built.softeningPc.toExponential(2)} ${tex("pc")} (default ${SOFTENING_FRACTION})`,
        );
      if (stepDivisor !== STEP_DIVISOR)
        parts.push(
          `${tex("dt")} = ${tex("tcr")}/${stepDivisor} = ` +
            `${(run.built.timeUnit / stepDivisor).toExponential(2)} Myr ` +
            `(default ${tex("tcr")}/${STEP_DIVISOR})`,
        );
      if (stepGuard) {
        parts.push(
          stepGuard.drops === 0
            ? "step shortens as the drift climbs, instead of the run stopping"
            : `step shortened ${stepGuard.drops}x to dt/${1 / stepGuard.scale} at t/t_cr ` +
              stepGuard.events
                .map((e) => (e.at / run!.built.timeUnit).toFixed(1))
                .join(", "),
        );
      }
      el.hidden = parts.length === 0;
      el.innerHTML = parts.length
        ? `${parts.join(" · ")} — not this page's defaults; the note below the figure describes those`
        : "";
    };

    const syncPlay = () => {
      playBtn.textContent = playing ? "❙❙ pause" : "▶ run";
      playBtn.classList.toggle("is-active", playing);
      /*
       * The line says WHAT IS HAPPENING, not what mode is selected — the toggle
       * beneath it already shows the mode, and a hint that only restated it was
       * the reason "frozen as drawn" meant nothing to anyone but its author.
       */
      /* Kept to ONE line at the panel's width. The longer forms of these wrapped,
         and a wrapped status line costs the figure a whole row of height — which
         is the scarcest thing in this layout. The toggle beside it already says
         which mode is selected, so the phrase only has to say what is happening. */
      /* HALTED IS NOT PAUSED. The play handler returns early once `halted` is set, so
         "press run" pointed at a button that does nothing — the banner said the run had
         ended while the line under it invited you to resume. */
      hint.textContent = scrubIndex !== null
        ? "looking back — drag right for live, or press Escape"
        : halted
        ? "stopped — reset or draw again"
        : playing
          ? pairConfirmed
            ? "hard binary — clock slowed"
            : gravity
              ? "every star pulls on every other"
              : "no gravity — stars keep going"
          : "paused — press run";
    };

    const rebuild = () => {
      stop();
      run = build();
      /* A new run is not the old run's binary. Clearing all three together is deliberate:
         leaving `halted` set would make the fresh run refuse to start, and leaving `pair` set
         would keep the clock slowed for a cluster that has no hard pair in it. */
      halted = false;
      pair = null;
      pairFrame = 0;
      /* `reset()` inside, so the segment counter goes back to zero too — a fresh run's binary
         must draw in the first colour, not wherever the last run's exchanges left off. */
      releasePair();
      /* A new run has no binary to follow, and leaving this set would frame the fresh cluster
         on a pair that no longer exists. */
      followPair = false;
      pairTrail = [];
      zoomPairBtn.textContent = "show me";
      hideAlert();
      hidePairStats();
      syncPairTab(); // the fresh run has no binary, so the tab goes with the old one
      /* Read off the BUILD, and only here. `built.timeUnit` is `crossingTime(state)`
         of the initial configuration — the same quantity the monitor divides by for
         `tCross` — so the displayed unit and the clock counted in it come from one
         number and cannot drift apart. It is a property of the draw, so it is set
         on rebuild rather than sampled every frame. */
      /* A fresh guard per run: its scale is a statement about THIS trajectory, and carrying one
         over would start a new cluster at a step it had not earned. */
      stepGuard = autostep
        ? createStepGuard({ tolerance: ENERGY_TOLERANCE })
        : null;
      setStat("tc0", run.built.timeUnit.toFixed(3));
      /*
       * Read off the BUILD, so the badge states the softening the force model is actually
       * using rather than the fraction that was asked for.
       */
      syncStepNote();
      /* Frozen here and nowhere else: "born" means the draw this run started from, so it is
         captured once per rebuild and never recomputed while the run proceeds. */
      bornIMF = run.built.identity ? toIMFHistogram(latentStars(), run.built.identity) : null;
      boundFlags = null;
      mfCounts = null;
      mfStale = true;
      setStat("legend-born", bornIMF ? String(run.built.state.n) : "—");
      hist = [];
      histPos = [];
      scrubIndex = null;
      mountField();
      hi0 = null;
      lo0 = null;
      /* Reset, new draw, an N change and a gravity switch all mean "start over",
         and the VIEW is part of the state you started over from — a zoom left
         from the last look would otherwise silently reframe the new run. */
      points?.setZoom(1);
      tSim = 0;
      storeEvery = 0;
      lastStored = -Infinity;
      worstSinceStore = 0;
      playing = false;
      sample();
      redraw();
      syncPlay();
      syncScheme();
      syncUrl();
    };

    playBtn.addEventListener("click", () => {
      /*
       * Run RUNS THE CLOCK. It does not choose the physics.
       *
       * It has been wrong in both directions. First it was DISABLED while gravity
       * was off, so the page's primary action did nothing on arrival and gave no
       * reason why. The fix for that was to have it silently switch gravity on —
       * which then overrode the reader's own choice, and contradicted the page
       * body's instruction to start with gravity off and watch. Now that "off"
       * integrates something worth watching, run needs to do neither.
       */
      /* A halted run cannot be resumed by pressing play — the state it stopped in is one the
         integrator has said it cannot advance honestly, so the only way on is reset or a new
         draw. Saying that is better than a button that appears to work and does nothing. */
      if (halted) return;
      /* Pressing run while looking back means "carry on from the live run", not "resume from
         here" — nothing was rewound, so there is no here to resume from. */
      if (scrubIndex !== null) setScrub(null);
      playing = !playing;
      syncPlay();
      playing ? start() : stop();
    });

    /*
     * ZOOM TO THE PAIR. The banner says a binary has formed; this is what lets a reader
     * actually look at it — at r_h ~ 0.65 pc and a ~ 0.03 pc the pair is a fraction of a
     * pixel in the default frame, so "a hard binary formed" is otherwise a claim about
     * something invisible.
     *
     * It reframes on the pair's own centre and a few times its semi-major axis. The scale bar
     * reports the new span, which is what keeps a moving frame honest here exactly as it does
     * for the reader's own scroll-zoom.
     */
    zoomPairBtn.addEventListener("click", () => {
      if (!pair || !run) return;
      followPair = !followPair;
      /* A fresh look starts a fresh trail: carrying the old one over would draw an orbit the
         pair has since left, and after an exchange it would not even be the same pair. */
      pairTrail = [];
      zoomPairBtn.textContent = followPair ? "back" : "show me";
      if (!followPair) {
        /* Leaving follow mode has to restore the SCALE too. `maxR` is sticky, so returning
           the centre alone would leave the reader at the binary's ~0.1 pc frame looking at a
           cluster four hundred times wider than the view. */
        points?.setFraming({ radiusPc: run.built.viewPc });
        points?.setZoom(1);
        requestAnimationFrame(updateScaleBar);
        return;
      }
      const st = run.built.state;
      const { i, j } = pair;
      const mi = st.mass[i];
      const mj = st.mass[j];
      const mT = mi + mj;
      const centre: [number, number, number] = [
        (st.pos[i * 3] * mi + st.pos[j * 3] * mj) / mT,
        (st.pos[i * 3 + 1] * mi + st.pos[j * 3 + 1] * mj) / mT,
        (st.pos[i * 3 + 2] * mi + st.pos[j * 3 + 2] * mj) / mT,
      ];
      /* 3a, not 4a: the pair's separation swings between periapsis and apoapsis, and the
         apoapsis of a bound orbit is at most 2a, so 3a holds the whole orbit with margin
         while still filling the frame with it. */
      points?.setFraming({ centre, radiusPc: Math.max(3 * pair.semiMajorAxis, 1e-4) });
      /*
       * rAF, exactly as the wheel listener does, and for the same reason: `setFraming` only
       * raises `reframe`, and the renderer recomputes `pxPerPc` inside `syncSize()` on its
       * NEXT tick. Reading it synchronously gets the previous framing's value, so the picture
       * zooms and the bar goes on reporting the old span — measured, it still read "2 pc"
       * after a zoom to a 0.12 pc frame. A scale bar that disagrees with the picture is worse
       * than none, because it is the thing that makes a moving frame honest.
       */
      requestAnimationFrame(updateScaleBar);
    });

    $<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-reset]").addEventListener("click", rebuild);
    $<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-reseed]").addEventListener("click", () => {
      seed += 1;
      rebuild();
    });

    function setGravity(on: boolean) {
      gravity = on;
      for (const o of gravBtns) {
        const active = (o.dataset.grav === "on") === on;
        o.classList.toggle("is-active", active);
        o.setAttribute("aria-pressed", String(active));
      }
      syncPlay();
    }

    /*
     * CHANGING THE PHYSICS RESTARTS THE RUN, from the same seed and therefore the
     * same initial conditions.
     *
     * It did not, and that produced two wrong pictures at once. The natural path
     * through this page is: arrive with gravity off, press run, watch the cluster
     * disperse, then switch gravity on. Before this:
     *
     *  - Every free-streaming sample stored `e: NaN`, so the energy trace began
     *    only where gravity was switched on while the x-axis still spanned from
     *    t = 0 — the trace occupied a sliver at the late end of an otherwise empty
     *    plot, which is what "the data does not persist" looked like. The record
     *    was complete; the run it described had simply not started yet.
     *
     *  - Worse, `energyDrift` is |E - E0|/|E0| against the energy captured when
     *    the monitor was built. Free streaming moves every star with no force
     *    integration, so the potential changes while the kinetic energy does not:
     *    the total is genuinely different by the time gravity comes on, and the
     *    "drift" then reported was that difference rather than any integrator
     *    error. A trust readout measuring the wrong thing is worse than none.
     *
     *  - And the r_h curves were normalised to hi0/lo0 from the free-streaming
     *    phase, so the two modes were spliced into one trace as though they were
     *    one experiment.
     *
     * Restarting makes each mode a controlled experiment against identical ICs,
     * which is exactly what the page's own copy asks the reader to do ("switch to
     * N-body and run it again from the start"). The clock keeps running if it was
     * running, so flipping the toggle plays the other experiment immediately
     * rather than making you press run again.
     */
    function selectGravity(on: boolean) {
      if (on === gravity) return;
      const wasPlaying = playing;
      setGravity(on); // mode + button state; rebuild touches neither
      rebuild(); // same seed => same initial conditions; fresh monitor, clock and history
      if (wasPlaying) {
        playing = true;
        syncPlay();
        start();
      }
    }

    for (const b of gravBtns) {
      b.addEventListener("click", () => selectGravity(b.dataset.grav === "on"));
    }

    nInput.addEventListener("input", () => {
      nVal.value = nInput.value;
    });
    nInput.addEventListener("change", rebuild);
    /* `input` updates the readout continuously; `change` rebuilds ONCE on release.
       Rebuilding per input event would re-sample and re-integrate a fresh cluster
       for every pixel of slider travel. */
    for (const b of root.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-scheme]")) {
      b.addEventListener("click", () => {
        const next = (b.dataset.scheme as "fsi4" | "logh" | "hermite") ?? "fsi4";
        if (next === scheme) return;
        scheme = next;
        for (const o of root.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-scheme]")) {
          const on = o === b;
          o.classList.toggle("is-active", on);
          o.setAttribute("aria-pressed", String(on));
        }
        /* A different integrator is a different run, not a continuation: the
           monitor's baseline and the whole error history belong to the scheme
           that produced them. */
        rebuild();
        syncScheme();
      });
    }
    alphaInput.addEventListener("input", () => {
      alphaVal.value = Number(alphaInput.value).toFixed(1);
    });
    alphaInput.addEventListener("change", rebuild);
    rhInput.addEventListener("input", () => {
      rhVal.value = Number(rhInput.value).toFixed(2);
    });
    rhInput.addEventListener("change", rebuild);
    gammaInput.addEventListener("input", () => {
      gammaVal.value = Number(gammaInput.value).toFixed(1);
    });
    gammaInput.addEventListener("change", rebuild);


    /*
     * The "?" notes, census's pattern. Open one at a time, close on Escape or on
     * a click outside — a tooltip that can only be dismissed by finding the same
     * small button again is a trap, and there are two of them on this page.
     */
    const infoBtns = Array.from(root.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-info]"));
    const closeInfo = (except?: string) => {
      for (const b of infoBtns) {
        const key = b.dataset.info!;
        if (key === except) continue;
        b.setAttribute("aria-expanded", "false");
        const pop = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>(`[data-popover="${key}"]`);
        if (pop) pop.hidden = true;
      }
    };
    /*
     * Place a fixed-position note against its button, flipping above when there is
     * no room below and clamping to the viewport on both axes. Fixed coordinates
     * are viewport coordinates, which is exactly what `getBoundingClientRect`
     * returns, so no scroll offset enters into it.
     */
    const placePopover = (b: HTMLElement, pop: HTMLElement) => {
      const r = b.getBoundingClientRect();
      const m = 8;
      const w = pop.offsetWidth;
      const h = pop.offsetHeight;
      const left = Math.min(Math.max(m, r.right - w), window.innerWidth - w - m);
      const below = r.bottom + 6;
      const top = below + h > window.innerHeight - m ? Math.max(m, r.top - h - 6) : below;
      pop.style.left = `${Math.round(left)}px`;
      pop.style.top = `${Math.round(top)}px`;
    };

    for (const b of infoBtns) {
      b.addEventListener("click", (e) => {
        e.stopPropagation();
        const key = b.dataset.info!;
        const pop = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>(`[data-popover="${key}"]`);
        if (!pop) return;
        const open = pop.hidden;
        closeInfo(key);
        pop.hidden = !open;
        b.setAttribute("aria-expanded", String(open));
        if (open) placePopover(b, pop); // measurable only once it is not hidden
      });
    }
    document.addEventListener("click", () => closeInfo());
    /* A fixed note does not travel with its button, so anything that moves the
       button closes it rather than leaving it stranded mid-page. */
    window.addEventListener("scroll", () => closeInfo(), { passive: true });
    window.addEventListener("resize", () => closeInfo());
    document.addEventListener("keydown", (e) => {
      if (e.key === "Escape") closeInfo();
    });

    /* Zooming changes the scale with no physics step behind it, and while paused
       nothing else would redraw the bar. rAF so the renderer's own wheel handler
       has already applied the new zoom by the time this reads pxPerPc. */
    field.addEventListener("wheel", () => requestAnimationFrame(updateScaleBar), { passive: true });
    /* rAF for the same reason the wheel handler uses one: `setZoom` only raises `reframe`, and
       `pxPerPc` is recomputed on the renderer's next tick. Reading it synchronously would give
       the previous framing's scale. */
    zoomInput?.addEventListener("input", () => {
      points?.setZoom(10 ** Number(zoomInput.value));
      requestAnimationFrame(updateScaleBar);
    });

    /* Tabs swap the SERIES, the legend, the y-title and the note together — one
       state, four things that must agree, so they are set in one place. */
    /* One path for a click and for a link. When the URL could select a tab the click handler
       could not, the two drifted immediately — this is the same reason `selectGravity` exists. */
    const selectTab = (tab: typeof plotTab) => {
      /* A link may name the binary tab, and a binary takes several crossing times to form —
         so arriving on it before there is one falls back rather than showing an empty frame
         with no way to tell whether it is broken or merely early. */
      plotTab = tab === "binary" && !pairConfirmed ? "radii" : tab;
      {
        for (const o of root.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-plot-tab]")) {
          const on = o.dataset.plotTab === tab;
          o.classList.toggle("is-active", on);
          o.setAttribute("aria-pressed", String(on));
        }
        for (const el of root.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>("[data-legend]"))
          el.hidden = el.dataset.legend !== plotTab;
        for (const el of root.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>("[data-ytitle]"))
          el.hidden = el.dataset.ytitle !== plotTab;
        /* Space-separated: two tabs share the time axis, one has its own. */
        for (const el of root.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>("[data-xtitle]"))
          el.hidden = !(el.dataset.xtitle ?? "").split(" ").includes(plotTab);
        for (const el of root.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>("[data-note]"))
          el.hidden = el.dataset.note !== plotTab;
        /* The cadence above only fires while the clock runs; arriving on the tab paused must
           still show the current state rather than the last one cached. */
        mfStale = true;
        drawPlots();
      }
    };
    for (const b of root.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-plot-tab]")) {
      b.addEventListener("click", () => {
        selectTab((b.dataset.plotTab as typeof plotTab) ?? "radii");
        syncUrl();
      });
    }

    /* ── export ────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
    /*
     * A filename that IDENTIFIES THE RUN. A folder of "dynamics.png" is a folder of files
     * nobody can tell apart six months later; the state that produced it is already the URL,
     * so the stem carries the same fields. This is the same argument the URL codec makes,
     * applied to a file.
     */
    const stem = (): string => {
      const bits = [`n${nInput.value}`, `seed${seed}`, scheme];
      if (softeningFraction !== SOFTENING_FRACTION) bits.push(`eps${softeningFraction}`);
      if (stepDivisor !== STEP_DIVISOR) bits.push(`dt${stepDivisor}`);
      if (autostep) bits.push("autostep");
      return `cluster-${bits.join("-")}`;
    };
    const download = (blob: Blob, filename: string): void => {
      const url = URL.createObjectURL(blob);
      const a = document.createElement("a");
      a.href = url;
      a.download = filename;
      a.click();
      /* Revoked on the next tick, not immediately: the click is synchronous but the fetch the
         browser makes for it is not, and revoking first gives an empty file. */
      setTimeout(() => URL.revokeObjectURL(url), 0);
    };

    $<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-save-png]").addEventListener("click", () => {
      /* `toBlob` on the WebGPU canvas snapshots what is on screen — including the trail and
         the current framing, which is what makes it worth putting in a talk. */
      field.toBlob((blob) => {
        if (blob) download(blob, `${stem()}-t${tSim.toFixed(1)}Myr.png`);
      }, "image/png");
    });

    /*
     * CSV OF THE TAB YOU ARE LOOKING AT, with the same decimation the plot has.
     *
     * It exports `hist`, which is thinned by the pairwise merge in sample() — so the file is
     * exactly what is drawn and not a secretly finer record. A header line says so, because a
     * student handed a CSV will reasonably assume it is the raw output.
     */
    const toCSV = (): { text: string; name: string } => {
      const meta =
        `# /explore/dynamics — ${stem()}\n` +
        `# t_cr = ${run?.built.timeUnit.toFixed(4) ?? "?"} Myr, ` +
        `eps = ${run?.built.softeningPc.toExponential(3) ?? "?"} pc, dt = t_cr/${stepDivisor}\n` +
        `# history is decimated by pairwise merge as the run grows: rows are the plotted ` +
        `samples, not every step\n`;
      if (plotTab === "mf") {
        const rows = (bornIMF?.bins ?? []).map((b, i) =>
          [b.logMlo, b.logMhi, b.logMc, b.count, mfCounts?.[i] ?? "", b.expected].join(","),
        );
        return {
          text: `${meta}logM_lo,logM_hi,logM_centre,count_born,count_bound,count_expected\n${rows.join("\n")}\n`,
          name: `${stem()}-massfunction.csv`,
        };
      }
      if (plotTab === "binary") {
        const rows = pairTrack.map((p) =>
          [p.t, p.a, p.e, p.hard, p.seg].join(","),
        );
        return {
          text: `${meta}t_Myr,a_pc,eccentricity,hardness,pair_segment\n${rows.join("\n")}\n`,
          name: `${stem()}-binary.csv`,
        };
      }
      /* The three time series share a file: they share an x axis, and a reader comparing them
         should not have to join three downloads on it. */
      const rows = hist.map((p) =>
        [p.t, p.hi, p.lo, p.bm, p.bn, p.l20, p.l50, p.l90, p.e].join(","),
      );
      return {
        text:
          `${meta}t_Myr,rh_heavy_over_rh0,rh_light_over_rh0,bound_mass_frac,bound_number_frac,` +
          `r20_pc,r50_pc,r90_pc,worst_abs_dE_over_E\n${rows.join("\n")}\n`,
        name: `${stem()}-series.csv`,
      };
    };

    $<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-save-csv]").addEventListener("click", (e) => {
      e.stopPropagation(); // the document listener that closes popovers
      const { text, name } = toCSV();
      download(new Blob([text], { type: "text/csv;charset=utf-8" }), name);
    });

    /* ── keyboard ──────────────────────────────────────────────── */
    /*
     * SPACE run/pause, R reset, N new draw — for presenting, and for reaching the transport
     * without a pointer.
     *
     * IT MUST NOT STEAL A KEY THAT BELONGS TO A CONTROL. A slider takes arrow keys and Home/End,
     * a button takes Space and Enter, and a text field takes everything — so a handler that
     * fired regardless would make the sliders unusable from the keyboard, which is worse than
     * having no shortcuts at all. Anything focused inside a form control, and any modifier
     * combination the browser or the OS owns, is left alone.
     */
    document.addEventListener("keydown", (e) => {
      if (e.altKey || e.ctrlKey || e.metaKey) return;
      const el = e.target as HTMLElement | null;
      const tag = el?.tagName;
      if (
        tag === "INPUT" ||
        tag === "TEXTAREA" ||
        tag === "SELECT" ||
        tag === "BUTTON" ||
        el?.isContentEditable
      )
        return;
      /* Only while the engine is on screen: a reader further down the page pressing space
         expects to scroll, and hijacking that is the same class of theft. */
      if (!onScreen) return;
      if (e.key === " ") {
        e.preventDefault(); // or the page scrolls as well as the run starting
        playBtn.click();
      } else if (e.key === "r" || e.key === "R") {
        rebuild();
      } else if (e.key === "n" || e.key === "N") {
        seed += 1;
        rebuild();
      }
    });

    /* ── the timeline ──────────────────────────────────────────── */
    const scrubInput = $<HTMLInputElement>("[data-scrub]");
    const scrubVal = $<HTMLOutputElement>("[data-scrub-val]");

    /*
     * The slider's range IS the record, so it grows with the run and coarsens with it. Rebuilt
     * on every draw rather than on a timer: the history halves itself without warning, and a
     * slider still offering 600 positions into a 300-sample record would land the reader on
     * samples that no longer exist.
     */
    const syncScrubRange = (): void => {
      const max = Math.max(0, histPos.length - 1);
      scrubInput.max = String(max);
      scrubInput.disabled = max === 0;
      if (scrubIndex === null) scrubInput.value = String(max);
    };

    const setScrub = (idx: number | null): void => {
      scrubIndex = idx;
      if (idx === null) {
        scrubVal.value = "live";
        /*
         * RESTORE THE READOUTS FROM THE LIVE STATE, through `sample()` rather than by hand.
         *
         * Scrubbing overwrites the clock and the legends, and returning to live left them
         * showing the moment that had been scrubbed to — measured: the clock read 0.3 Myr on a
         * run that was at 1.2. The run itself was never wrong; the numbers describing it were.
         *
         * `sample()` is the one path that writes all of them, so calling it cannot leave one
         * behind the way a hand-written restore would. It does not push a duplicate history
         * sample: tSim has not moved, so the store interval is not due.
         */
        sample();
      } else {
        const p = hist[idx];
        scrubVal.value = p ? `${p.t.toFixed(2)} Myr` : "—";
        /* The readouts must follow, or the legend keeps describing the present while the
           picture shows the past — the disagreement this control exists to remove. */
        setStat("rhi", "—");
        setStat("rlo", "—");
        if (p) {
          setStat("legend-bm", Number.isFinite(p.bm) ? `${(p.bm * 100).toFixed(0)}%` : "—");
          setStat("legend-bn", Number.isFinite(p.bn) ? `${(p.bn * 100).toFixed(0)}%` : "—");
          driftEl.textContent = Number.isFinite(p.e) ? p.e.toExponential(1) : "—";
          clockT.textContent = `t = ${p.t.toFixed(1)} Myr`;
          /* Same split as the live path: the symbol is typeset, only the number is written.
             Setting textContent here would have wiped the KaTeX out of the chip for good. */
          clockTcr.hidden = !run;
          if (run) setStat("tcr-now", (p.t / run.built.timeUnit).toFixed(1));
        }
      }
      syncPlay();
      redraw();
    };

    scrubInput.addEventListener("input", () => {
      const max = Number(scrubInput.max);
      const idx = Number(scrubInput.value);
      /* Scrubbing pauses. Letting the run continue underneath would move the record out from
         under the slider while it is being dragged. */
      if (playing) {
        playing = false;
        stop();
      }
      setScrub(idx >= max ? null : idx);
    });

    document.addEventListener("keydown", (e) => {
      /* Any other keydown handler will have returned by now if a control had focus. */
      if (e.key === "Escape" && scrubIndex !== null) setScrub(null);
    });

    window.addEventListener("resize", redraw);

    /*
     * THE ADDRESS IS READ BEFORE THE FIRST BUILD, because `build()` takes its values from the
     * INPUTS. Setting `seed` and the scheme but leaving the sliders at their markup defaults
     * would produce a cluster that matched the URL in some fields and the HTML in others —
     * the shared-link failure that is hardest to notice, because the picture still looks
     * plausible.
     *
     * The readouts beside each slider are set here too: they are written by `input` events,
     * which assigning `.value` does not fire.
     */
    {
      const st = decode(URL_SCHEMA, location.search);
      seed = st.seed;
      nInput.value = String(st.n);
      nVal.value = nInput.value;
      rhInput.value = String(st.rh);
      rhVal.value = st.rh.toFixed(2);
      gammaInput.value = String(st.gamma);
      gammaVal.value = st.gamma.toFixed(1);
      alphaInput.value = String(st.alpha);
      alphaVal.value = st.alpha.toFixed(1);
      scheme = st.scheme;
      softeningFraction = st.eps;
      stepDivisor = st.steps;
      autostep = st.autostep;
      for (const o of root.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-scheme]")) {
        const on = o.dataset.scheme === scheme;
        o.classList.toggle("is-active", on);
        o.setAttribute("aria-pressed", String(on));
      }
      setGravity(st.gravity);
      selectTab(st.tab);
    }
    rebuild();
  }
</script>

<style>
  /*
   * NARROWER THAN CENSUS'S 76rem, and for a reason census does not have: this
   * page carries a full-width energy strip UNDER the two square panels, and the
   * whole point of that strip is to be readable at the same time as the cluster
   * it vouches for. At 76rem the grid stood 1018 px against a 900 px viewport.
   * The columns set the square canvases, so narrowing the root is what brings the
   * strip back onto the same screen — everything scales together rather than one
   * panel being singled out.
   */
  .xpl-dyn {
    display: grid;
    /* 76rem, not 64. The 64 was chosen to bring the energy strip inside a 900 px viewport —
       but 900 px was the TESTING convention from CLAUDE.md, not anyone's screen, and the
       machine this is authored on reports 1512x857. Widening buys bigger viewscreens and puts
       the four plot tabs back on one row; it does not fix the vertical squeeze, which is a
       separate problem and needs a separate answer. */
    max-inline-size: 76rem;
    margin-inline: auto;
  }
  /* Tighter than census's --space-m. This figure stacks four bands (cluster +
     plot, console, energy strip) where census stacks two, so the gaps are the
     cheapest 25 px available and cost nothing a reader would notice. */
  /*
   * THE RECEIPT SITS BESIDE THE INSTRUMENT, NOT BELOW IT.
   *
   * The energy strip's whole justification is that it vouches for the cluster above it — and
   * stacked full-width it could not, because it fell off the bottom of the screen. Measured at
   * 1512x857, the machine this is authored on: the grid stood 1122 px in 660 px of space below
   * the header, and the strip ENDED at y = 1281. A reader had to scroll away from the thing
   * being certified in order to read the certificate.
   *
   * So the cluster spans both rows of column one and the strip takes column two under the
   * plot. Same information, no extra band: measured after, the grid is ~820 px and the strip
   * sits level with the cluster it is about.
   *
   * Fitting EVERYTHING above the fold is not available at this height and chasing it was the
   * error — the header alone takes ~197 px. Getting the cluster and its receipt onto one
   * screen together is the requirement, and that is what this does.
   */
  .xpl-dyn__grid {
    display: grid;
    /*
     * THE CLUSTER IS A FIXED 550 px CANVAS; the plots take what is left.
     *
     * It was briefly squeezed to 525 to fit the four plot tabs on one row. That trade is off:
     * the tab set is going to keep growing, so no column width wins that race, and two rows of
     * tabs is the accepted answer. Fixing the cluster instead means the frame a reader watches
     * stops moving every time a tab is added — which is worth more than a tidy caption.
     *
     * 552 px, not 550: the panel's 1 px borders come out of the column, and the canvas is what
     * the number is about.
     */
    grid-template-columns: 552px minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--space-s);
    align-items: stretch; /* each row's two cells match height → columns align */
  }
  .xpl-dyn__panel--field {
    grid-column: 1;
    grid-row: 1 / 3;
  }
  /*
   * IN FULL SCREEN THE CANVAS FILLS THE SCREEN, so the aspect ratio has to go. Everywhere
   * else the frame is width-driven — that is what keeps the grid's height predictable — but
   * here the height IS the available space, and a 1:1 canvas would letterbox itself on a wide
   * display and waste exactly the room this button was pressed to get.
   */
  .xpl-dyn__panel--field:fullscreen,
  .xpl-dyn__panel--field.is-expanded {
    border: 0;
    border-radius: 0;
    background: var(--bg-void);
  }
  /* The permission-free half: a fixed overlay covering the window. */
  .xpl-dyn__panel--field.is-expanded {
    position: fixed;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 60;
  }
  .xpl-dyn__panel--field:fullscreen .xpl-dyn__stage,
  .xpl-dyn__panel--field.is-expanded .xpl-dyn__stage {
    flex: 1;
    min-block-size: 0;
  }
  .xpl-dyn__panel--field:fullscreen [data-field],
  .xpl-dyn__panel--field.is-expanded [data-field] {
    aspect-ratio: auto;
    inline-size: 100%;
    block-size: 100%;
  }
  .xpl-dyn__panel--energy {
    grid-column: 2;
    grid-row: 2;
  }
  .xpl-dyn__console {
    grid-row: 3;
  }
  /*
   * Census's panel treatment, read from its stylesheet rather than remembered.
   * The note that used to sit here claimed census's figure panels are
   * "TRANSPARENT with zero padding"; they are not — `.xpl-census__panel` carries
   * a radial-gradient background and `overflow: hidden`, and that mistaken note
   * is why these two pages never looked like siblings.
   */
  .xpl-dyn__panel {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    background: radial-gradient(
      120% 120% at 50% 0%,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--bg-surface) 92%, black),
      var(--bg-void)
    );
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-m);
    overflow: hidden;
    transition: border-color var(--dur-base) var(--ease-out);
  }
  /* Flat surface, not the gradient — census's control panel is the one box that
     reads as a console rather than an instrument. */
  .xpl-dyn__panel--pad {
    padding: var(--space-s);
    background: var(--bg-surface);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-s);
  }
  .xpl-dyn__panel--strip {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
  }
  /* Row 1 of column two, beside the top of the cluster. */
  .xpl-dyn__grid > .xpl-dyn__panel:not([class*="--"]) {
    grid-column: 2;
    grid-row: 1;
  }
  /* Census's caption bar: a titled header rule, not a floating line of text. */
  .xpl-dyn__panel figcaption {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--space-2xs);
    padding: var(--space-2xs) var(--space-s);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--ink);
    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--border);
  }
  .xpl-dyn__panel figcaption span {
    font-size: var(--step--2);
    color: var(--ink-faint);
  }
  /* ── legend + "?" popover, census's `data-info` pattern ── */
  /*
   * THE TAB GROUP TAKES ITS OWN LINE. It was wrapping to two rows even after the panel got
   * wider, because it shares a `flex-wrap` row with the legend and was wrapping against THAT
   * rather than against the panel edge — which is why widening never fixed it. Given the whole
   * line, four descriptive labels fit on one.
   */
  .xpl-dyn__panel figcaption > .xpl-dyn__modes {
    flex-basis: 100%;
    /*
     * Tighter than the other mode groups, and scoped to the plot tabs so the transport, gravity
     * and integrator groups keep their original proportions. It was originally the last 4.6 px
     * needed to fit four labels on one row; the tab set has since been allowed to wrap, so what
     * it buys now is simply more tabs per row as that set grows.
     */
    gap: 2px;
  }
  .xpl-dyn__panel figcaption > .xpl-dyn__modes button {
    padding-inline: 0.55em;
  }
  .xpl-dyn__keys {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-2xs);
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }
  .xpl-dyn__legend {
    position: relative;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-2xs);
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }
  .xpl-dyn__key {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.4em;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  /* Drawn from the SAME tokens the canvas reads through css(), so the key cannot
     disagree with the line it labels. Dashes match drawSeries's [4, 3]. */
  .xpl-dyn__swatch {
    inline-size: 1.4em;
    block-size: 0;
    border-block-start-width: 2px;
    flex: none;
  }
  .xpl-dyn__swatch--hi {
    border-block-start-style: solid;
    border-color: var(--spec-rose-bright);
  }
  .xpl-dyn__swatch--lo {
    border-block-start-style: dashed;
    border-color: var(--spec-teal-bright);
  }
  .xpl-dyn__swatch--e {
    border-block-start-style: solid;
    border-color: var(--spec-amber);
  }
  /* The mass-function tab draws BARS, so its keys are filled blocks rather than the
     line strokes above — a 2px rule is the wrong glyph for an area. */
  .xpl-dyn__swatch--born,
  .xpl-dyn__swatch--kept {
    block-size: 0.7em;
    border: 0;
    border-radius: 1px;
  }
  .xpl-dyn__swatch--born {
    background: var(--ink-faint);
  }
  .xpl-dyn__swatch--kept {
    background: var(--spec-teal-bright);
  }
  /*
   * THE BREACH FLAG, ON A SELECTOR THAT MATCHES.
   *
   * This was `.xpl-dyn__val.is-bad`, and `xpl-dyn__val` appeared nowhere in the
   * markup — only here, twice. So the rule matched nothing and the flag has never
   * been visible: `sample()` sets `is-bad` from the monitor's latched `healthy`
   * every frame, and the readout went on rendering in `--ink` as though the run
   * were fine. Confirmed live on seed 2028, which latches at t/t_cr = 13.8 —
   * `classList.contains('is-bad')` was true while the computed colour was still
   * white.
   *
   * The class survived last session's rewrite of the readout into the console's
   * `<dl>`; the class name it depended on did not. That is the failure mode a
   * styling hook has and a data attribute does not, so this now hangs off the
   * one the script actually sets.
   */
  .xpl-dyn__stats .is-bad {
    color: var(--spec-rose-bright);
  }
  .xpl-dyn__info {
    flex: none;
    inline-size: 1.3rem;
    block-size: 1.3rem;
    padding: 0;
    border-radius: 50%;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    background: transparent;
    color: var(--ink-faint);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--step--2);
    line-height: 1;
    cursor: pointer;
  }
  .xpl-dyn__info:hover,
  .xpl-dyn__info[aria-expanded="true"] {
    color: var(--spec-teal-bright);
    border-color: var(--spec-teal-bright);
  }
  /* Pops OVER the panel, never through it: the plot keeps its height whether the
     note is open or closed, which is the whole reason this is not a paragraph. */
  /* Anchored so a control's note can open UPWARD: the sliders sit at the bottom
     of the panel, where a downward popover would fall off the figure. */
  .xpl-dyn__ctrl--noted {
    position: relative;
  }
  /* The note button rides on the label line rather than taking a grid row. */
  .xpl-dyn__ctrl--noted .xpl-dyn__info {
    position: absolute;
    inset-block-start: -2px;
    inset-inline-end: 0;
  }
  /*
   * FIXED, and positioned by JS — see `placePopover`.
   *
   * Absolute positioning put it inside `.xpl-dyn__panel`, which carries
   * `overflow: hidden` to clip the canvas to the border radius. Measured on the
   * concentration note: the popover ran from x=31 to x=447 while its panel began
   * at x=208, so 177 px of it was cut off. An earlier check of mine tested only
   * whether the TOP edge escaped and reported it fine, which is why this shipped.
   *
   * Fixed takes it out of the panel's clipping context altogether, so no note can
   * ever be trimmed by the box it happens to live in — and the placement can then
   * flip and clamp against the VIEWPORT, which is the thing that actually
   * constrains it.
   */
  /*
   * A POP-OVER MUST FIT ON THE SCREEN, and until the scheme note grew a comparison table
   * nothing here was long enough to prove it did not. Measured at 1440x900: that note came out
   * 1279 px tall in a 900 px viewport, so its last third — the part explaining why the default
   * is what it is — was simply unreachable.
   *
   * `max-block-size` plus `overflow-y: auto` bounds it and lets the rest be scrolled to. The
   * 2rem leaves room for the JS placement to sit it clear of the viewport edge.
   */
  .xpl-dyn__popover {
    position: fixed;
    z-index: 20;
    inline-size: min(26rem, 70vw);
    max-block-size: calc(100vh - 2rem);
    overflow-y: auto;
    overscroll-behavior: contain;
    padding: var(--space-2xs) var(--space-s);
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--spec-teal) 30%, var(--border));
    border-radius: var(--radius-m);
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--bg-surface) 94%, black);
    box-shadow: 0 8px 24px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.45);
    font-size: var(--step--2);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    line-height: 1.5;
    text-align: start;
    white-space: normal;
  }

  .xpl-dyn__panel canvas {
    display: block;
    inline-size: 100%;
    touch-action: none;
  }
  /*
   * BOTH viewscreens are square, as census's cluster and H–R are. That is what
   * removes the dead band under the radii plot: at 4/3 the plot panel was
   * ~290 px shorter than the field beside it, and `align-items: stretch` turned
   * the difference into empty panel rather than into plot.
   */
  /*
   * SQUARE, AND IT STAYS SQUARE FOR FREE.
   *
   * This panel spans BOTH rows of column one, and those rows are sized by column two — the
   * plot and the strip, whose heights come from their own aspect ratios. So the cluster's
   * height is not what drives the grid: anything up to the span costs nothing.
   *
   * Two attempts here shrank it to 5:4 and then 4:3 on the assumption that its height was the
   * expensive thing. Measured afterwards, restoring the square left the grid at 848 px
   * unchanged — the saving came entirely from moving the strip into column two, and the
   * cluster had been cropped for nothing.
   */
  .xpl-dyn__panel [data-field] {
    aspect-ratio: 1;
    block-size: auto;
  }
  /*
   * The radius plot GROWS to fill its row instead of holding a fixed square.
   * The field panel beside it now carries the metrics strip and the sliders too,
   * so a fixed-square plot would be the shorter cell and `stretch` would turn the
   * difference into empty panel — the dead band this page already had once.
   */
  .xpl-dyn__panel [data-radii] {
    block-size: 100%;
  }
  .xpl-dyn__panel [data-field] {
    cursor: grab;
  }
  .xpl-dyn__panel [data-field]:active {
    cursor: grabbing;
  }
  /* The frame the HUD is positioned against — the canvas box, not the panel, so
     the controls cannot drift over the caption rule. */
  .xpl-dyn__stage {
    position: relative;
  }
  .xpl-dyn__hud {
    position: absolute;
    inset-inline: var(--space-2xs);
    z-index: 2;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-2xs);
    pointer-events: none; /* the canvas keeps the drag; only the controls take clicks */
  }
  .xpl-dyn__hud--top {
    inset-block-start: var(--space-2xs);
    justify-content: space-between;
  }
  /* Bottom-LEFT, where census puts its scale bar: the cluster is centred and
     roughly circular, so the corners are the only space it never occupies. */
  .xpl-dyn__hud--bottom {
    inset-block-end: var(--space-2xs);
    justify-content: space-between;
    align-items: flex-end;
  }
  .xpl-dyn__viewctl {
    display: grid;
    justify-items: end;
    gap: var(--space-3xs, 4px);
  }
  /* Short enough to stay out of the cluster's way; the track is the only wide part. */
  .xpl-dyn__zoom {
    inline-size: 7rem;
    accent-color: var(--spec-teal-bright);
    cursor: pointer;
  }
  .xpl-dyn__scale {
    display: grid;
    justify-items: end;
    gap: 2px;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--step--2);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    text-shadow: 0 1px 3px var(--bg-void);
  }
  .xpl-dyn__scale-bar {
    display: block;
    block-size: 0;
    border-block-start: 2px solid var(--ink-soft);
    border-inline: 1px solid var(--ink-soft);
    block-size: 6px;
  }
  .xpl-dyn__hud > * {
    pointer-events: auto;
  }
  /* Legible over a star field of unknown brightness: the readout gets the same
     panel-tinted plate census gives its H–R card, rather than bare text that
     disappears the moment a bright star drifts under it. */
  .xpl-dyn__clock-sub {
    display: block;
    text-align: end;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .xpl-dyn__hud .xpl-dyn__clock {
    padding: 0.15em 0.6em;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--bg-surface) 88%, black);
    color: var(--ink);
    font-size: var(--step--2);
  }
  /* 12:1 left no room for tick labels; the strip is small but it is a plot. */
  /*
   * The strip is a receipt, so it stays short — but not so short that it cannot
   * be READ. 16:1 was an overcorrection: with the 12/32 px axis margins it left
   * 52 px of plotting area, which is too little to tell a bounded wobble from a
   * slow climb, and that distinction is the only thing the panel is for. 9:1 is
   * what it started at and pushed the strip off the same screen as the cluster.
   *
   * Now 20:3 — half again as tall as the 10:1 it settled at — because the axis is
   * no longer autoscaled to the trace: it is pinned to the monitor's tolerance, so
   * a healthy run sits low in a frame whose top is the limit. That headroom IS the
   * reading, and it needs room to be one.
   */
  /*
   * 4:1 NOW IT IS HALF THE WIDTH. The 20:3 was set when this strip ran the full 1216 px and
   * gave a readable 182 px; in a 596 px column the same ratio is 89 px, and with the axis
   * margins that leaves 43 px of plotting area for five decade labels — they collided.
   *
   * 9:2 rather than 4:1 because the grid's height is set by column two's WIDTH — both canvases
   * are aspect-driven, so gridH is about 417 + 0.75 x W2. At W2 = 596 that is 864 against a
   * 857 px viewport; 9:2 gives the strip 132 px (86 px of plotting area, twice what it had)
   * and brings the grid to ~847. Measured, not guessed — the first two attempts here assumed
   * the plot would absorb the change and it did not.
   */
  .xpl-dyn__panel [data-energy] {
    aspect-ratio: 9 / 2;
    inline-size: 100%;
    block-size: auto;
  }
  /*
   * Axis titles exactly as census sets them: the y-title absolutely positioned
   * and rotated over the canvas's own left margin, both at --step-0.
   *
   * They were at --step--2 here — two steps below census — while the tick labels
   * silently fell back to the canvas default. So the whole axis furniture was
   * smaller than the diagram it was labelling, on a plot that carries the page's
   * central claim.
   */
  .xpl-dyn__plot {
    position: relative;
    flex: 1 1 auto;
    min-block-size: 14rem;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
  }
  .xpl-dyn__plot > canvas {
    flex: 1 1 auto;
    min-block-size: 0;
  }
  /* The energy strip's wrapper. It only exists to carry the two axis titles, so
     it must NOT bring the tall plot's `min-block-size: 14rem` with it — the strip
     sizes itself from its canvas's 20:3 ratio and a floor would stretch it. */
  .xpl-dyn__plot--strip {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    min-block-size: 0;
  }
  .xpl-dyn__plot--strip > canvas {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
  }
  /* --step--1, a step BELOW the tick labels' 16px rather than above it. The title
     names the axis once; the tick values are read repeatedly and against the
     curve, so they are the thing that has to be legible. This was --step-0, which
     put the furniture above the numbers in the visual hierarchy. */
  /* 0.9em on top of --step--2. KaTeX applies its own internal sizing, so a title set at the
     same token as the surrounding text renders a step larger than it; em is the right unit
     because the correction is RELATIVE to whatever the scale hands down, and check-type allows
     em for exactly that reason. --step--2 is already the floor of the scale, so there is
     nothing smaller to reach for. */
  .xpl-dyn__xtitle .math,
  .xpl-dyn__ytitle .math {
    font-size: 0.9em;
  }
  .xpl-dyn__xtitle {
    display: block;
    text-align: center;
    padding-block: 0.2rem 0.35rem;
    /* Matched to the y-title. These were a step apart, and the x-title read visibly
       larger than the label rotated beside it. */
    font-size: var(--step--2);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .xpl-dyn__ytitle {
    position: absolute;
    /* 1.15rem, not census's 0.85rem: rotated, this label's height becomes its
       width, and `r_h(t)/r_h(0)` has subscripts that make it 32px tall against
       census's `L/L_\odot`. At 0.85rem it overran the panel edge and `overflow:
       hidden` clipped it — measured, left 733 against a panel starting at 734. */
    inset-inline-start: 1.15rem;
    inset-block-start: 50%;
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%) rotate(-90deg);
    transform-origin: center;
    line-height: 1;
    /* One step down. These are labels ON a figure, not body copy, and at --step--1 the
       rotated y-title was competing with the tick labels it sits beside. */
    font-size: var(--step--2);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    white-space: nowrap;
    pointer-events: none;
  }
  /*
   * The binary banner. Sits ON the canvas, between the two HUD rows, because vertical space
   * is what this layout is short of — a banner in the flow would cost a row on every run,
   * including the ones that never form a binary.
   *
   * `pointer-events: none` on the box with `auto` on the button: the banner covers a slab of
   * the cluster, and drag-to-orbit has to keep working through it.
   */
  /*
   * Styled as a HUD CHIP, not as an alert box.
   *
   * The first version was a full-width slab with a solid amber border and a filled amber
   * pill, sitting directly under the transport row — Anna: "that pop-up looks horrible". It
   * was loud in a panel whose whole visual language is quiet monospace chips, and it crowded
   * the controls it sat beneath.
   *
   * Now: bottom-anchored, above the gravity row rather than under the transport one; content
   * width rather than full width; the same surface, border and radius as every other chip on
   * this canvas, with amber carried by ONE word and a dot instead of a frame. The button
   * borrows `.xpl-dyn__modes button`'s outline styling for the same reason.
   */
  .xpl-dyn__alert {
    position: absolute;
    inset-inline-start: var(--space-2xs);
    inset-block-end: 2.8rem;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.5em;
    inline-size: fit-content;
    max-inline-size: calc(100% - 2 * var(--space-2xs));
    padding: 0.3em 0.75em;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--bg-void) 82%, transparent);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--step--2);
    line-height: 1.5;
    pointer-events: none;
  }
  .xpl-dyn__alert[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }
  /* The one amber thing, plus a dot — enough to read as a notice without a frame around it. */
  .xpl-dyn__alert strong {
    color: var(--spec-amber);
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .xpl-dyn__alert strong::before {
    content: "● ";
    font-size: 0.8em;
  }
  .xpl-dyn__alert-btn {
    pointer-events: auto;
    flex: none;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--step--2);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    background: var(--bg-surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 999px;
    padding: 0.1em 0.7em;
    cursor: pointer;
  }
  .xpl-dyn__alert-btn:hover {
    color: var(--spec-teal-bright);
    border-color: var(--spec-teal-bright);
  }
  .xpl-dyn__alert-btn[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }
  /*
   * The scheme comparison table. The first table to appear in one of these pop-overs, and it
   * earns the format: three schemes x four epochs is a grid, and the point — that two of them
   * PLATEAU while the third keeps climbing — is only readable when the columns line up.
   * Monospace figures so 3.0e-4 and 6.1e-5 can be compared by eye down a column.
   */
  .xpl-dyn__cmp {
    inline-size: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    margin-block: var(--space-2xs);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    /*
     * Measured at 1440: this table wanted 503 px inside a 416 px pop-over and pushed its own
     * right-hand columns out of the box. `table-layout: fixed` is what actually bounds it —
     * it makes the columns share the available width instead of sizing to content.
     *
     * The font stays at --step--2. My first attempt was --step--3, which DOES NOT EXIST: the
     * scale stops at --step--2, and `check:type` passed anyway because it validates the
     * `--step-*` pattern rather than that the token resolves. `font-size: var(--step--3)` is
     * an invalid declaration that silently inherits — the same shape of failure as the dead
     * `.xpl-dyn__val` selector and the `var()` inside `ctx.font`.
     */
    font-size: var(--step--2);
    table-layout: fixed;
  }
  .xpl-dyn__cmp th,
  .xpl-dyn__cmp td {
    padding: 0.15em 0.25em;
    text-align: right;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .xpl-dyn__cmp th:first-child,
  .xpl-dyn__cmp td:first-child,
  .xpl-dyn__cmp th:nth-child(2),
  .xpl-dyn__cmp td:nth-child(2) {
    text-align: left;
  }
  .xpl-dyn__cmp thead th {
    color: var(--ink-faint);
    font-weight: 400;
    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--border);
  }
  .xpl-dyn__cmp tbody td:first-child {
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .xpl-dyn__hint {
    display: block;
    padding: 0 var(--space-s) var(--space-2xs);
    margin-block-start: var(--space-3xs);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--ink-faint);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
  /* The softening override. Amber rather than faint, because its whole job is to stop the
     paragraph under the figure being read as true of THIS run — a note nobody notices would
     not do that. No font-size of its own: it inherits the hint's --step--1. */
  .xpl-dyn__hint--eps {
    color: var(--spec-amber, #fbbf24);
    padding-block-end: var(--space-2xs);
  }
  /* The plot's hint is still an explanation of what two curves mean, so it keeps
     its measure and its reading colour; only the field's is a status phrase. */
  .xpl-dyn__panel--strip .xpl-dyn__hint,
  .xpl-dyn__plot ~ .xpl-dyn__hint {
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    padding-block-end: var(--space-xs);
  }
  .xpl-dyn__clock {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
  }
  .xpl-dyn__toolbar,
  .xpl-dyn__lenses,
  .xpl-dyn__modes,
  .xpl-dyn__seg {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-3xs);
    align-items: center;
  }
  .xpl-dyn__toolbar {
    justify-content: space-between;
  }
  .xpl-dyn__lenses button,
  .xpl-dyn__modes button,
  .xpl-dyn__seg button,
  .xpl-dyn__reseed {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--step--2);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    background: var(--bg-surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 999px;
    padding: 0.25em 0.7em;
    cursor: pointer;
  }
  .xpl-dyn__lenses button.is-active,
  .xpl-dyn__modes button.is-active,
  .xpl-dyn__seg button.is-active {
    color: var(--bg-void);
    background: var(--spec-teal-bright);
    border-color: var(--spec-teal-bright);
  }
  .xpl-dyn__lenses button:disabled,
  .xpl-dyn__modes button:disabled {
    opacity: 0.45;
    cursor: not-allowed;
  }
  .xpl-dyn__lenshd {
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin-block-end: var(--space-2xs);
  }
  .xpl-dyn__note {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-2xs);
    font-size: var(--step--2);
    color: var(--ink-faint);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
  /* Two knobs on one row, label and slider side by side rather than stacked —
     the stacked form with its `small` caption was four lines for two controls,
     and this panel now has to hold the frame as well. */
  /*
   * NOT six equal columns. The integrator cell holds two pill buttons and a "?"
   * rather than a slider, and it is the one cell whose content has a hard minimum:
   * measured at 1440 px, six equal columns gave every cell 141 px while
   * "FSI4 | Hermite | ?" needs about 145, so the toggles wrapped to a second line
   * and the whole console grew a row.
   *
   * The four sliders give the width up, because they have it to give: r_h is the
   * greediest at 94 steps of travel, and at 136 px that is still ~1.4 px a step.
   * Slider travel degrades smoothly; a wrapped button row is a step change in the
   * panel's height.
   */
  /* 1.9fr, up from 1.4: the integrator cell went from two pill buttons to THREE when LogH
     joined, and at 1.4fr "FSI4 | LogH | Hermite | ?" needed ~197 px in a ~190 px cell, so it
     wrapped again — the same failure this rule was written to fix, reintroduced by adding a
     scheme. Speed gives up the width; it is a 16-step slider and the least travel-hungry. */
  .xpl-dyn__controls {
    display: grid;
    /* Four sliders, the timeline, then the integrator — WIDTHS FOLLOW THE DOM ORDER, which is
